"""Original Grade 8 NC ELA reading-standard packets (RL.8.x and RI.8.x).

Same format as rl_ri_grade7_content.py: original passages written for a more
mature (8th-grade) reading level, plus a 20-item Q&A bank per standard with
reasoning in every answer. No released-test or third-party material is used.
Standard codes and text are the Common Core Reading Literature / Reading
Informational Text standards for grade 8, which North Carolina's Standard
Course of Study adopts under the same numbering.
"""
NC = 'https://www.thecorestandards.org/ELA-Literacy/'
ROWS = []


def add(slug, title, code, standard_text, objective, lesson, models, texts, items, price):
    assert len(items) == 20, (slug, len(items))
    assert len(models) == 2
    assert len(texts) in (1, 2), (slug, len(texts))
    strand = 'RL' if code.startswith('RL') else 'RI'
    num = code.split('.')[-1]
    ROWS.append(dict(id='nc-g8-' + slug, title=title, state='North Carolina', grade='8', subject='Reading',
        standard=code + ' — ' + standard_text, objective=objective,
        source=NC + strand + '/8/' + num + '/', resourceType='Complete ELA practice packet',
        lesson=lesson, models=models, texts=texts,
        items=[{'q': q, 'answer': a, 'work': a} for q, a in items], price=price))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.1
add('rl81-textual-evidence', 'Evidence and Inference Lab', 'RL.8.1',
 'Cite the textual evidence that most strongly supports an analysis of what the text says explicitly as well as inferences drawn from the text.',
 'Cite the textual evidence that most strongly supports an analysis of explicit statements and inferences drawn from a literary text.',
 'Explicit evidence is stated directly in the text; an inference is a reasonable conclusion built from evidence plus what a reader already knows. At grade 8, "most strongly supports" means choosing the single best piece of evidence, not just any evidence that fits — weigh competing details and explain why one supports the analysis better than another.',
 [('Paragraph 2 says Marcus "read the acceptance letter four times, then folded it into his back pocket instead of showing anyone." What is the strongest evidence for an inference that he is unsure how to feel about the news?',
   'The detail that he read it four times but told no one is the strongest evidence — rereading suggests he is still processing the news, and folding it away instead of sharing it suggests he is not ready to react publicly, which together support uncertainty rather than simple excitement or disappointment.'),
  ('Between "the gym was hot" and "she wiped her palms on her shorts three times before the serve," which detail more strongly supports an inference about nervousness, and why?',
   'The second detail more strongly supports nervousness because it is a specific, repeated physical action tied directly to the moment of pressure, while "the gym was hot" is a general environmental fact that could support many different inferences.')],
 [('The Acceptance Letter', (
   '1. Marcus had applied to the magnet arts high school without telling his older brother, Dante, who had been rejected from the same program two years earlier and had not mentioned it since. Marcus kept his own application folded inside a library book so it would not turn up in a drawer.\n\n'
   '2. The letter came on a Tuesday, while Dante was at practice. Marcus read the acceptance letter four times, then folded it into his back pocket instead of showing anyone. He went to his room and closed the door, which he almost never did.\n\n'
   '3. At dinner, their mother asked if anything interesting had happened that day. Marcus said no. Dante, half-listening, asked to be passed the rice. Marcus passed it and said nothing else for the rest of the meal.\n\n'
   '4. Over the next week, Marcus carried the letter with him — to school, to the bus stop, once even into the shower, where it sat on the sink until he remembered and moved it. He did not read it again. He also did not throw it away.\n\n'
   '5. On Saturday, Dante found him in the garage, sitting on an overturned bucket, the letter open on his knee. "You\'ve had that a week," Dante said. It was not a question. Marcus had not heard him come in.\n\n'
   '6. "I didn\'t want it to be a thing," Marcus said. "You didn\'t get in, and I—" He stopped. Dante sat down on a second bucket, close enough that their shoulders nearly touched.\n\n'
   '7. "I got in your way for two years without meaning to," Dante said. "Read it out loud. I want to hear how it sounds when it\'s actually happening to someone." Marcus looked at him for a long moment before he understood that Dante meant it.\n\n'
   '8. Marcus read the letter aloud, once, in the garage, with the door up and the evening light coming in sideways across the concrete. When he finished, Dante didn\'t say anything about his own rejection at all. He just said, "You should tell Mom tonight," and meant it kindly.\n\n'
   '9. That night at dinner, Marcus told her. Dante, across the table, was the first to smile.'))],
 [('What does paragraph 1 state explicitly about how Marcus applied to the magnet school?', 'He applied without telling Dante, and kept the application folded inside a library book so it would not be found.'),
  ('What is the strongest evidence that Marcus is anxious about how Dante will react to the news? Cite it and explain why it is the strongest choice.', 'The strongest evidence is that he hid the application inside a library book (paragraph 1) and later hid the acceptance letter in his pocket instead of telling anyone (paragraph 2); these two deliberate acts of concealment, both aimed at avoiding Dante finding out, more strongly support anxiety than a single detail like closing his door would alone.'),
  ('What explicit action does Marcus take with the letter in paragraph 2?', 'He reads it four times, then folds it into his back pocket instead of showing anyone, and closes his bedroom door.'),
  ('Which detail from paragraph 3 most strongly supports an inference that Marcus is deliberately hiding something at dinner, and why is it stronger than the fact that he "said no"?', '"Marcus... said nothing else for the rest of the meal" is stronger, because staying unusually silent for an entire meal is a sustained behavior change, while a single "no" answer alone could simply mean nothing happened that day.'),
  ('What explicit detail in paragraph 4 shows Marcus cannot decide what to do with the letter?', 'He carries it everywhere for a week, including into the shower, but does not read it again and does not throw it away.'),
  ('What can you infer about why Marcus does not throw the letter away, even though he also will not talk about it? Cite the strongest evidence.', 'He likely still wants to keep the acceptance real to himself even while afraid to share it; the strongest evidence is that he carries it everywhere, including into the shower (paragraph 4), which shows the letter matters enough to stay physically close even without being discussed.'),
  ('What explicit statement in paragraph 5 shows Dante already suspected something?', '"You\'ve had that a week," Dante said — stated as fact, not a question, showing he already knew or had guessed.'),
  ('Between "It was not a question" and "Marcus had not heard him come in," which more strongly supports the inference that Dante had been quietly paying attention to Marcus all week? Explain.', '"It was not a question" more strongly supports it, because it shows Dante already had enough information to state a fact rather than ask, which requires him to have been observing Marcus over time, while "Marcus had not heard him come in" only shows Marcus was distracted in that moment.'),
  ('What explicit reason does Marcus give in paragraph 6 for hiding the letter?', 'He says he "didn\'t want it to be a thing," and starts to reference Dante not getting in before stopping himself.'),
  ('What can you infer about Dante\'s feelings toward his own rejection from paragraph 7? Cite the strongest evidence.', 'He seems to have made peace with it or wants to face it directly rather than avoid it; the strongest evidence is his direct request, "Read it out loud. I want to hear how it sounds when it\'s actually happening to someone," which shows he is choosing to engage with the painful topic rather than avoid it.'),
  ('What explicit physical detail in paragraph 7 shows a shift toward closeness between the brothers?', 'Dante sits down on a second bucket, close enough that their shoulders nearly touch.'),
  ('Which is the stronger evidence that Dante has genuinely moved past resentment: sitting close to Marcus, or asking him to read the letter aloud? Explain your reasoning.', 'Asking Marcus to read the letter aloud is stronger evidence, because it is an active choice to face the source of possible resentment directly, while sitting close is a smaller, more passive gesture that could occur without resolving deeper feelings.'),
  ('What explicit detail describes the setting as Marcus reads the letter aloud in paragraph 8?', 'The garage door is up and the evening light is coming in sideways across the concrete.'),
  ('What can you infer from Dante saying nothing about his own rejection after Marcus finishes reading? Cite evidence.', 'It suggests Dante is intentionally keeping the moment focused on Marcus rather than making it about himself; paragraph 8 states he "didn\'t say anything about his own rejection at all" immediately after the reading, which is a deliberate omission given the topic\'s relevance.'),
  ('What explicit words does Dante use to encourage Marcus at the end of paragraph 8?', '"You should tell Mom tonight."'),
  ('What is the strongest evidence in the whole story that Dante\'s support is sincere rather than reluctant? Justify your choice.', 'The strongest evidence is that Dante is "the first to smile" when Marcus tells their mother (paragraph 9); a genuine, immediate positive reaction at the emotional climax carries more weight than his earlier words alone, since actions in the story\'s final moment confirm what his words suggested.'),
  ('Explain the difference between explicit and inferred information using two examples from this story.', 'Explicit: the text directly states Marcus read the letter four times (paragraph 2). Inferred: the reader must conclude from his silence and hiding behavior that he feels anxious or unsure, since the story never states his emotions directly.'),
  ('A classmate claims the story is only about sibling rivalry. Using the strongest textual evidence, explain why that analysis is incomplete.', 'The story is more about reconciliation than rivalry; the strongest evidence against a rivalry-only reading is Dante\'s statement, "I got in your way for two years without meaning to" (paragraph 7), which shows him taking responsibility and moving toward connection rather than continuing to compete.'),
  ('Identify one place in the story where two different inferences could reasonably be drawn from the same evidence, and explain both.', 'Dante finding Marcus in the garage (paragraph 5) could support either "Dante has been quietly worried about Marcus" or "Dante happened to walk in by chance"; the story leans toward the first because Dante already knows how long Marcus has had the letter, which is unlikely to be coincidence.'),
  ('Write one full inference about Dante, and identify the single strongest piece of evidence that supports it, explaining why it beats at least one other possible piece of evidence.', 'Answers vary; require a clear inference about Dante plus one specific cited detail, with an explanation of why that detail supports the inference more strongly than another available detail.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.1
add('ri81-evidence-informational', 'Strongest Evidence Workshop', 'RI.8.1',
 'Cite the textual evidence that most strongly supports an analysis of what the text says explicitly as well as inferences drawn from the text.',
 'Cite the textual evidence that most strongly supports an analysis of explicit statements and inferences drawn from an informational text.',
 'In informational text, explicit evidence often appears as stated facts, data, or direct quotations from sources. An inference in informational text is a reasonable conclusion drawn from combining facts, not a claim the text states outright. At grade 8, choosing the "strongest" evidence means comparing multiple possible pieces of support and explaining why one is more precise, more directly relevant, or more reliable than another.',
 [('A report states "the bridge was inspected in 2019" and separately "engineers recommended repairs in March 2024 after finding corrosion in support cables." Which detail more strongly supports an inference that the bridge\'s condition worsened over time, and why?',
   'The second detail is stronger, because it directly identifies a specific problem (corrosion) and a specific recommendation for repair, while the first detail alone only establishes a date and says nothing about the bridge\'s condition.'),
  ('If a text states a company\'s profits and separately quotes an employee saying morale was low, which is stronger evidence for an inference about employee experience, and why?',
   'The employee quote is stronger for an inference specifically about employee experience, because it comes from a direct source describing that experience, while profit figures describe financial results and only indirectly relate to how employees felt.')],
 [('Second Chances: How One City Rebuilt Its Public Library System (Part 1)', (
   '1. In 2015, the city of Millbrook faced a decision: renovate its aging central library or close two of its three branches to cut costs. The central library, built in 1962, had not been substantially updated in over fifty years. Its roof leaked in four places, and its basement archive had flooded twice in the previous decade.\n\n'
   '2. City council member Rosa Ibarra argued against closures. "A library is not a luxury," she told the council in a recorded session. "For some families, it is the only place with reliable internet, air conditioning in summer, and a quiet room to study." Her statement was backed by a city survey showing that 38 percent of library visitors used the building for internet access alone.\n\n'
   '3. Opponents of renovation pointed to cost. Estimates from the city engineer placed the renovation at $4.2 million, compared to an estimated $600,000 in annual savings from closing two branches. At that rate, opponents argued, renovation would take seven years just to break even.\n\n'
   '4. The council commissioned an independent study in 2016. The study found that library usage had actually increased by 22 percent over the previous five years, driven largely by after-school programs and job-search workshops for adults. It also found that the two branches proposed for closure served neighborhoods with the city\'s lowest average household income.')),
  ('Second Chances: How One City Rebuilt Its Public Library System (Part 2)', (
   '5. In 2017, the council voted 5–2 to renovate rather than close branches, funding the project through a mix of municipal bonds and a state library-improvement grant. Construction began in 2018 and was completed in early 2020, just before a period when library usage nationally would shift significantly due to public health measures.\n\n'
   '6. By 2023, three years after renovation, Millbrook\'s library system reported that overall visits across all three branches had risen 41 percent compared to 2015 levels. The after-school program alone served over 900 students that year, more than triple its 2015 enrollment of 280.\n\n'
   '7. Rosa Ibarra, no longer on the council but still a frequent visitor, was asked in a 2023 newspaper interview whether the investment had been worth it. She pointed not to the usage statistics but to a single detail: the renovated building now had four public meeting rooms, all booked solid most weeknights, used by tutoring groups, citizenship-class students, and a monthly repair café where residents fixed broken appliances instead of throwing them away.'))],
 [('What does paragraph 1 state explicitly about the library\'s physical condition before renovation?', 'The roof leaked in four places, and the basement archive had flooded twice in the previous decade.'),
  ('Which piece of evidence most strongly supports the inference that low-income families would be most affected by branch closures, and why is it stronger than other available evidence?', 'The finding in paragraph 4 that the branches proposed for closure served neighborhoods with the city\'s lowest average household income is strongest, because it directly and specifically connects closure to income level, unlike Rosa Ibarra\'s general statement about libraries being important, which does not specify which neighborhoods would be affected.'),
  ('What explicit statistic does the city survey provide about internet access, according to paragraph 2?', '38 percent of library visitors used the building for internet access alone.'),
  ('What is the strongest evidence for an inference that renovation opponents were focused mainly on short-term cost rather than long-term community impact? Explain your choice.', 'The strongest evidence is that opponents calculated renovation would "take seven years just to break even" (paragraph 3), a purely financial comparison; this is stronger than simply noting they opposed renovation, because it shows their argument was built specifically around a break-even cost calculation rather than community outcomes.'),
  ('What explicit finding did the 2016 independent study report about library usage trends?', 'Library usage had increased by 22 percent over the previous five years.'),
  ('Between the 22 percent usage increase and the finding about low-income neighborhoods, which more strongly supports the council\'s eventual decision to renovate rather than close branches? Justify your answer.', 'The finding about low-income neighborhoods is somewhat stronger for justifying renovation specifically over closure, because it identifies a direct equity consequence of closing branches, while the usage increase shows the library was valuable but does not by itself argue against closing the two lower-use branches.'),
  ('What explicit vote result is stated in paragraph 5?', 'The council voted 5–2 to renovate rather than close branches.'),
  ('What can you infer about the timing of the renovation\'s completion from paragraph 5? Cite the strongest evidence.', 'The renovation was completed at a fortunate time, just before increased public reliance on libraries; the strongest evidence is the phrase "just before a period when library usage nationally would shift significantly due to public health measures," which directly signals a timing connection without stating the outcome explicitly.'),
  ('What explicit statistic does paragraph 6 give about overall visit growth by 2023?', 'Overall visits across all three branches had risen 41 percent compared to 2015 levels.'),
  ('Which is stronger evidence that the after-school program specifically drove the increase in usage: the 22 percent five-year increase from 2016, or the tripling of after-school enrollment mentioned in paragraph 6? Explain.', 'The tripling of after-school enrollment (from 280 to over 900 students) is stronger, because it isolates one specific program\'s growth, while the 22 percent figure is a broader usage statistic that could be caused by many different factors, not just after-school programs.'),
  ('What explicit detail does Rosa Ibarra point to in paragraph 7 when asked if the investment was worth it?', 'The four public meeting rooms, booked solid most weeknights with tutoring groups, citizenship-class students, and a repair café.'),
  ('Why might Rosa Ibarra\'s choice to cite meeting-room usage, rather than the 41 percent visit increase, be considered strong evidence of her personal view? Explain using the text.', 'It suggests she values the library\'s role as a community gathering space more than raw visitor numbers; the text explicitly says she "pointed not to the usage statistics but to" the meeting rooms, showing a deliberate choice to emphasize a different kind of evidence than the official statistics.'),
  ('Explain the difference between an explicit fact and an inference using two examples from this article.', 'Explicit: the article directly states the renovation cost $4.2 million (paragraph 3). Inference: a reader must conclude that community programs, not just building repairs, drove the growth in usage, since the article never states this as a single direct claim but supports it with several related facts.'),
  ('A reader claims the article proves renovation was purely a financial success. Using the strongest evidence, explain why this claim oversimplifies the article.', 'The claim oversimplifies the article because the strongest evidence of impact, such as the growth in after-school enrollment and the daily use of meeting rooms, describes community and educational outcomes, not financial ones; the article never presents a direct financial return figure to support a claim of purely financial success.'),
  ('Which piece of evidence in the article is least useful for supporting an inference about community impact, and why?', 'The $4.2 million renovation estimate (paragraph 3) is least useful for that specific inference, because it is a cost figure that describes the investment itself rather than any outcome or effect on the community.'),
  ('Identify one place in the article where two different inferences could reasonably be drawn from the same evidence, and explain both.', 'The 5–2 council vote (paragraph 5) could support either "most council members were convinced by the equity findings" or "two members had unresolved concerns about cost"; both are reasonable, since the article states the vote but does not explain the two dissenting votes.'),
  ('What explicit detail from paragraph 4 identifies which programs drove the usage increase, and why is it stronger evidence than simply citing the 22 percent figure alone?', 'The article explicitly states the increase was "driven largely by after-school programs and job-search workshops for adults"; naming the specific programs is stronger evidence than the 22 percent figure alone, because it explains the cause of the growth rather than just reporting that growth happened.'),
  ('Why does the article include the detail that construction finished "just before" a period of national change in library use? What inference does this support?', 'It supports the inference that Millbrook\'s renovation was fortunately timed, which strengthens the case that the investment paid off, since the newly renovated building was ready right as broader demand for library services likely shifted.'),
  ('Compare the strength of Rosa Ibarra\'s 2015 survey statistic (paragraph 2) to the 2023 enrollment statistic (paragraph 6) as evidence for the renovation\'s long-term success. Which is stronger, and why?', 'The 2023 enrollment statistic is stronger evidence of long-term success, because it measures an actual outcome years after renovation was completed, while the 2015 survey statistic only measured need before the renovation happened and cannot by itself confirm the investment worked.'),
  ('Write one full inference about the value of the renovation to the community, and identify the single strongest piece of evidence that supports it, explaining why it beats at least one other candidate detail.', 'Answers vary; require a clear inference plus one specific cited detail, with an explanation of why that detail supports the inference more strongly than at least one other available detail from the article.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.2
add('rl82-theme-central-idea', 'Theme Through the Wreckage', 'RL.8.2',
 'Determine a theme or central idea of a text and analyze its development over the course of the text, including its relationship to the characters, setting, and plot; provide an objective summary of the text.',
 'Determine a theme of a literary text, analyze how it develops through its relationship to characters, setting, and plot, and write an objective summary.',
 'A theme is a message about life, stated as a full sentence, not a one-word topic. At grade 8, tracing a theme means showing its relationship to specific story elements: how a character\'s choices, the setting\'s pressures, and plot turns work together to develop it, not just naming a lesson at the end. An objective summary reports events in order without opinions or added judgment.',
 [('Topic: teamwork under pressure. Write a possible theme as a full sentence, and name one story element (character, setting, or plot) that might develop it.',
   'A possible theme: "Real teamwork means adapting the plan together, not defending your own idea." The plot — a sudden setback that forces the team to change course together — is the element most likely to develop this theme.'),
  ('Which sentence is an objective summary sentence, and which is an opinion: (a) "The robot\'s arm jammed during the final test," (b) "The team foolishly ignored the obvious wiring problem"?',
   'Sentence (a) is objective — it reports a fact without judgment. Sentence (b) is an opinion, because "foolishly" and "obvious" are the writer\'s judgments, not neutral facts.')],
 [('The Circuit Breaks (Part 1)', (
   '1. Priya had wired the claw mechanism four times, and four times it had worked in the practice garage. Two hours before the regional competition, it worked a fifth time, lifting a foam block cleanly onto the scoring platform while her teammates Malik and Deja cheered from across the room.\n\n'
   '2. Forty minutes before their first match, the claw stopped closing. Priya popped the access panel and found a wire scorched black where it should have been copper-bright. "Bad connector," she said. "I can rewire it, but not in forty minutes. Not alone."\n\n'
   '3. Malik wanted to swap in the backup claw design they had scrapped three weeks earlier for being too slow. Deja wanted to skip the claw task entirely and focus the match on driving points instead, which the robot could still score without a working arm. Priya wanted to keep rewiring and hope.\n\n'
   '4. They had six minutes left to decide when their coach, Mr. Ferris, said only, "Whatever you pick, pick it together, not three separate robots in one shell." He did not tell them which to choose.')),
  ('The Circuit Breaks (Part 2)', (
   '5. Priya put down her wire strippers. "The backup claw is slower, but it\'s reliable, and Deja\'s driving points still count if the claw fails mid-match anyway. We don\'t have to choose one plan. We can run the backup claw as backup, exactly like its name says, and lean on driving if it still doesn\'t work."\n\n'
   '6. Malik reinstalled the backup arm in under four minutes while Deja recalibrated the drive code for scoring without relying on any claw at all. Priya, instead of finishing her rewire, called out timing cues to both of them so their work would not collide inside the crowded chassis.\n\n'
   '7. The backup claw was slow, exactly as Malik had warned. In their first match, it lifted only one block, fumbling a second. But Deja\'s driving scored steadily the whole match, and when the claw did fumble, no one on the team stopped to blame it, because the whole plan had been built to survive that fumble.\n\n'
   '8. They did not win the regional. They placed ninth of twenty-two, high enough to be invited to the state qualifier. At the drive-home meeting, Priya still wanted to know why the connector had scorched. Malik wanted to redesign the claw entirely. Deja wanted to keep the driving code exactly as it was.\n\n'
   '9. "Three different repair lists," Mr. Ferris said, almost smiling, "and one robot that made it to state because none of you tried to run it alone at minute forty."'))],
 [('What explicit detail in paragraph 1 shows the claw mechanism had worked reliably before the competition?', 'It had worked five times total in testing, including once two hours before the competition, lifting a foam block cleanly.'),
  ('What is the topic of this story in a few words, and how is that different from a theme?', 'The topic is teamwork or robotics competition; a topic is just a subject, while a theme must be a full-sentence message about life that the story teaches through its events.'),
  ('What explicit problem does Priya find in paragraph 2, and how much time is left to fix it?', 'A wire is scorched black where it should be copper-bright, a bad connector, with about forty minutes left before the first match.'),
  ('How do the three teammates\' different proposals in paragraph 3 reflect their individual characters?', 'Priya wants to keep fixing her own work (persistence with her original plan), Malik wants to fall back on a known design (caution), and Deja wants to change strategy entirely (adaptability) — each proposal reflects a different way of responding to pressure.'),
  ('What does Mr. Ferris\'s statement in paragraph 4 suggest about the theme the story is building toward?', 'It suggests the theme will involve the difference between individual effort and true collaboration — he does not solve the problem for them, but insists the solution be a shared one.'),
  ('How does the setting (a competition with a hard time limit) shape the plot decision in paragraph 3?', 'The ticking clock removes the option of debating endlessly or trying all three ideas in sequence; the pressure of limited time forces the team to combine ideas quickly rather than pick just one.'),
  ('What plan does Priya propose in paragraph 5, and how does it differ from simply picking one teammate\'s idea?', 'She proposes combining the backup claw with Deja\'s driving-focused strategy as a safety net for each other, rather than choosing only Malik\'s idea or only Deja\'s idea alone.'),
  ('How does Priya\'s action in paragraph 6 (calling out timing cues instead of finishing her own rewire) show character development from paragraph 2?', 'In paragraph 2 she wanted to keep working on her own fix alone; by paragraph 6 she sets aside her individual task to support both teammates\' work, showing a shift toward collaboration.'),
  ('What explicit outcome does the backup claw have during the first match, according to paragraph 7?', 'It lifts only one block and fumbles a second one, performing slowly as expected.'),
  ('How does the team\'s reaction to the claw\'s fumble in paragraph 7 develop the story\'s theme?', 'No one blames the claw or each other for the fumble, because the plan was deliberately built to survive that kind of failure — this shows the theme that shared planning protects a team from a single point of failure.'),
  ('What explicit competition result is stated in paragraph 8?', 'They placed ninth of twenty-two teams, which was high enough to qualify for the state competition.'),
  ('How does paragraph 8 complicate a simple "teamwork always agrees" reading of the theme? Use evidence.', 'The team still disagrees afterward — Priya wants to investigate the connector, Malik wants to redesign the claw, and Deja wants to keep her code unchanged — showing the theme is not about eliminating disagreement, but about not acting on it alone under pressure.'),
  ('What does Mr. Ferris\'s final line in paragraph 9 suggest is the real reason the team succeeded?', 'It suggests they succeeded because they combined their different ideas into one shared plan at the critical moment, rather than because any one of their individual ideas was correct.'),
  ('State a theme for this story as a complete sentence, using a story element to support it.', 'Answers vary; must be a full-sentence life lesson, such as "A team survives a crisis by combining differing ideas rather than any one person acting alone," supported by referencing the plot moment when Priya combines the three proposals.'),
  ('Write one objective sentence summarizing paragraphs 1–2 (no opinions).', 'Priya\'s claw mechanism had worked reliably in testing, but forty minutes before their first match, she discovered a scorched connector had caused it to stop working.'),
  ('Identify one word in this draft summary that is an opinion, not a fact: "Priya bravely sacrificed her own fix to help her panicking teammates."', '"Bravely" and "panicking" are opinions/judgments about the characters\' emotional states and actions, not neutral facts stated in the text.'),
  ('Write an objective one-sentence summary of the story\'s ending (paragraphs 8–9).', 'The team placed ninth of twenty-two and qualified for the state competition, and their coach noted that combining their different ideas, rather than acting alone, was what got them there.'),
  ('Why is "the team\'s only good idea was Priya\'s combined plan" not appropriate for an objective summary?', 'It is an opinion and an overstatement; the text shows all three ideas contributed to the final plan, so crediting only Priya misrepresents what the story actually states.'),
  ('Combine paragraphs 5, 6, and 7 into one two-sentence objective summary.', 'Priya proposed combining the backup claw with Deja\'s driving-focused strategy, and Malik and Priya worked together to install and coordinate it in the remaining minutes. The backup claw performed slowly and fumbled once during the match, but the team\'s driving points kept them competitive.'),
  ('Trace how the theme develops from the team\'s disagreement in paragraph 3 to their shared success in paragraph 7, citing at least two moments.', 'In paragraph 3, the three teammates each push a separate plan; by paragraph 6, Priya sets aside her own repair to support the combined plan, and in paragraph 7, the team does not blame the claw\'s fumble because the plan was built to withstand it — together these moments trace a shift from separate individual plans to one shared, resilient plan.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.2
add('ri82-central-ideas-summary', 'Tracing the Central Idea', 'RI.8.2',
 'Determine a central idea of a text and analyze its development over the course of the text, including its relationship to supporting ideas; provide an objective summary of the text.',
 'Determine a central idea of an informational text, analyze how supporting ideas develop it across the text, and write an objective summary.',
 'A central idea is the main point an informational text develops, not just its topic. Supporting ideas are the smaller points, facts, or examples that build and refine the central idea as the text progresses. At grade 8, tracing development means showing how specific supporting ideas connect to and strengthen the central idea from one part of the text to another, not just restating the topic.',
 [('Topic: a city\'s new recycling program. Write a possible central idea as a full sentence, and name one type of supporting idea (a fact, statistic, or example) that might develop it.',
   'A possible central idea: "A city\'s new recycling program succeeded because it made participation easy for residents." A supporting idea could be a statistic showing participation rates before and after curbside bins were introduced.'),
  ('Is "the program collected 40 percent more recyclable material in its second year" an objective summary sentence or an opinion? Explain.',
   'It is objective — it reports a specific, verifiable statistic without added judgment; a subjective version might add a word like "impressively" or "finally."')],
 [('From Empty Lot to Open Market: How Two Neighbors Started Riverbend\'s Saturday Market (Part 1)', (
   '1. In 2019, the corner of Fifth and Poplar in Riverbend was an empty gravel lot used mostly for overflow parking during football games. By 2024, the same lot hosted a Saturday market drawing over 2,000 visitors a week and generating an estimated $340,000 a year for more than thirty small vendors. The central idea behind that change was simple: a market succeeds when it removes the specific barriers that keep small vendors from selling in the first place.\n\n'
   '2. The market began with two neighbors, Elena Vasquez and Tom Okafor, who ran small food businesses out of their homes but could not afford the vendor fees at the region\'s established farmers markets, which ranged from $75 to $150 per weekend. Vasquez and Okafor surveyed twelve other home-based sellers in Riverbend and found the same complaint from all twelve: high fees, not lack of demand, were keeping them from selling in public.\n\n'
   '3. Their first solution addressed that exact barrier. They approached the city about using the empty lot for free on Saturdays, arguing that an unused gravel lot generated zero tax revenue anyway. The city agreed to a six-month trial, waiving fees entirely and asking only that vendors carry basic liability insurance, which Vasquez and Okafor arranged as a discounted group policy for $40 a year per vendor.')),
  ('From Empty Lot to Open Market: How Two Neighbors Started Riverbend\'s Saturday Market (Part 2)', (
   '4. The second barrier they identified was visibility: even vendors who could afford to sell had no easy way to tell customers where or when. Vasquez built a simple text-alert list, and Okafor designed hand-painted signs for the five streets leading into the neighborhood. In the trial\'s first month, twelve vendors participated; by the sixth month, the number had grown to twenty-six, entirely by word of mouth and the text list, with no paid advertising.\n\n'
   '5. The third barrier surfaced only after the market was running: inconsistent hours. Some vendors opened at 8 a.m., others at 10, and customers who arrived expecting a full market sometimes found half the stalls empty. The market\'s informal vendor council, formed by the participants themselves in month four, voted to require a fixed 9 a.m. to 1 p.m. window for any vendor wanting a reserved spot.\n\n'
   '6. By the end of the six-month trial, the city\'s own data showed average Saturday foot traffic on Fifth and Poplar had risen from near zero to just over 2,000 visitors, and the city extended the arrangement indefinitely, still without vendor fees. Vasquez and Okafor now spend roughly five hours a week coordinating the market, work they describe as unpaid but far less time-consuming than the fee negotiations they used to face at other markets.\n\n'
   '7. Neither Vasquez nor Okafor has since raised a fee. When a city council member suggested a small fee to fund market improvements in 2023, the vendor council voted it down, 19 to 4, and instead proposed a voluntary $5 monthly contribution, which about half the vendors currently pay.'))],
 [('What explicit statistic does paragraph 1 give about the market\'s weekly visitors by 2024?', 'Over 2,000 visitors a week.'),
  ('What is the central idea stated explicitly in paragraph 1?', 'A market succeeds when it removes the specific barriers that keep small vendors from selling in the first place.'),
  ('What explicit barrier does paragraph 2 identify through the survey of twelve home-based sellers?', 'High vendor fees at established farmers markets, ranging from $75 to $150 per weekend, not a lack of customer demand.'),
  ('How does the fee-waiver solution in paragraph 3 directly support the central idea from paragraph 1?', 'It removes the specific barrier (high fees) identified in paragraph 2, directly matching the central idea that removing barriers, not simply creating a market, is what leads to vendor participation.'),
  ('What second barrier does paragraph 4 introduce, and how is it different from the fee barrier in paragraph 2?', 'The second barrier is visibility — customers not knowing where or when the market operated — which is different because it affects whether customers show up, not whether vendors can afford to participate.'),
  ('What explicit statistic in paragraph 4 shows the visibility solution\'s effect on vendor participation?', 'Vendor participation grew from twelve in the trial\'s first month to twenty-six by the sixth month.'),
  ('How does the vendor growth statistic in paragraph 4 develop the central idea further than the fee-waiver alone could?', 'It shows that removing one barrier (fees) was not enough by itself; solving the visibility barrier was also necessary for participation to keep growing, extending the central idea to include more than one kind of barrier.'),
  ('What third barrier does paragraph 5 identify, and who solved it?', 'Inconsistent vendor hours; the market\'s own informal vendor council, formed by the participants themselves, solved it by setting a fixed 9 a.m. to 1 p.m. window.'),
  ('Why is it significant for the central idea that the vendor council, not the city, solved the hours problem in paragraph 5?', 'It shows the barrier-removal approach extends to self-organization among vendors, not just outside city support, reinforcing that identifying and removing a specific barrier — regardless of who removes it — is what drives success.'),
  ('What explicit data does paragraph 6 give about foot traffic by the end of the trial?', 'Foot traffic rose from near zero to just over 2,000 visitors a week.'),
  ('How does paragraph 7\'s vote result (19 to 4) support the central idea established across the article?', 'The vendor council\'s strong vote against a new fee shows their continued belief that removing financial barriers, the very first barrier identified, remains central to the market\'s success, even years later.'),
  ('Which supporting idea most strongly develops the central idea: the fee waiver, the text-alert system, or the fixed hours rule? Justify your choice.', 'The fee waiver most strongly develops the central idea, because it was the original barrier identified through the founders\' survey and the direct basis for the central idea stated in paragraph 1; the other two solved barriers that emerged only after the market already existed.'),
  ('Explain how paragraphs 2 through 5 are organized to support the central idea, using the word "barrier" in your answer.', 'The paragraphs are organized as a sequence of barriers and their removal — fees, then visibility, then inconsistent hours — each paragraph identifying one specific barrier and the corresponding solution, which builds the central idea point by point rather than stating it once.'),
  ('Write one objective sentence summarizing paragraphs 2 and 3 (no opinions).', 'Vasquez and Okafor surveyed other home-based sellers, found that high vendor fees were the main barrier to selling, and arranged a fee-free trial with the city along with discounted liability insurance.'),
  ('Identify one word in this draft summary that is an opinion, not a fact: "The founders cleverly solved the visibility problem with simple, effective signs."', '"Cleverly" and "effective" are opinions about the quality of the solution, not neutral facts from the text.'),
  ('Write an objective one-sentence summary of paragraphs 6 and 7.', 'By the end of the trial, foot traffic had risen to over 2,000 visitors and the city extended the fee-free arrangement, which the vendor council later voted to keep by rejecting a proposed fee in 2023.'),
  ('Why would "the market grew because Riverbend residents love supporting local business" be an inappropriate central-idea statement for this article?', 'It is not supported as the article\'s central idea; the article specifically attributes the market\'s growth to the identification and removal of concrete barriers (fees, visibility, hours), not to general community sentiment, which the text never measures or claims.'),
  ('Combine paragraphs 1, 4, and 6 into a two-sentence objective summary tracing the central idea\'s development.', 'An empty gravel lot became a Saturday market after its founders identified and removed the specific barriers keeping small vendors from selling, starting with high fees and later addressing visibility. Vendor participation and customer foot traffic both grew steadily as each barrier was addressed, reaching over 2,000 weekly visitors by the end of the trial.'),
  ('What explicit detail from paragraph 2 shows the founders based their solution on evidence rather than a guess?', 'They surveyed twelve other home-based sellers and found the same complaint from all twelve, which grounded their solution in direct evidence rather than assumption.'),
  ('Trace the central idea\'s development across the entire article, citing at least three supporting ideas in the order they appear.', 'The central idea — that removing specific barriers drives a market\'s success — develops first through the fee waiver (paragraph 3), then through the visibility solutions of a text list and signage (paragraph 4), then through the vendor council\'s fixed-hours rule (paragraph 5), each addressing a different barrier the community had actually identified.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.3
add('rl83-dialogue-propels-action', 'The Line That Wasn\'t in the Script', 'RL.8.3',
 'Analyze how particular lines of dialogue or incidents in a story or drama propel the action, reveal aspects of a character, or provoke a decision.',
 'Analyze how specific lines of dialogue or a specific incident in a literary text propel the plot forward, reveal something about a character, or provoke another character\'s decision.',
 'At grade 8, this standard asks you to zoom in on one specific line or incident and explain its effect — not the scene in general. A line can do more than one job at once: it can reveal character (show something true about who they are) while also propelling action (causing what happens next) or provoking a decision (forcing another character to choose).',
 [('In a story, a character who has stayed calm all scene suddenly says, "I am so tired of being the reasonable one." What might this single line reveal about the character?',
   'It could reveal hidden frustration or resentment the character has been suppressing, suggesting their calm behavior earlier was a performance rather than their true feeling.'),
  ('If that same line is followed by another character walking out of the room, what has the line done in terms of plot?',
   'It has propelled the action — caused a specific event (the other character leaving) that likely would not have happened without that line being spoken.')],
 [('The Line That Wasn\'t in the Script (Part 1)', (
   '1. Aaliyah had played Officer Reyes in the school play for six weeks of rehearsal, and for six weeks the climactic scene had gone the same way: her character confronts DeShawn\'s character, Marcus, and Marcus delivers his scripted apology — "I know I was wrong, and I\'m ready to face it" — before the lights dim.\n\n'
   '2. At the final dress rehearsal, with the director and half the cast\'s families watching from the back row, DeShawn reached the line and stopped. He looked at Aaliyah, and instead of the scripted apology, he said, "I\'m not sorry. I\'d do it again, and you know why."\n\n'
   '3. The stage manager\'s script, taped open to that page, sat useless in the wings. Aaliyah had exactly the length of one held breath to decide whether to stay in character or break the scene to fix DeShawn\'s mistake.\n\n'
   '4. She chose to stay in character. "Then I guess we\'re done pretending you\'re the good guy," she said, a line that existed nowhere in the script, and stepped between Marcus and the door instead of letting him exit as blocked.')),
  ('The Line That Wasn\'t in the Script (Part 2)', (
   '5. The rest of the cast froze, unsure whether to follow the printed blocking or whatever was happening in front of them. DeShawn, still in character, grabbed a prop chair and turned it toward Aaliyah instead of exiting stage left as rehearsed. "You don\'t get to decide that," he said. "Nobody made you the judge."\n\n'
   '6. Backstage, the director, Ms.Okoye, did not call "cut," though her hand hovered near the small flashlight she used to signal a stop. She let the scene run four more unscripted lines before Aaliyah, breathing hard, delivered something close enough to the original ending that the lighting cue still made sense.\n\n'
   '7. Afterward, DeShawn apologized to the cast for going off-script without warning anyone, admitting he had been carrying real frustration from an argument with his own older brother that afternoon and it had "come out sideways" during the scene. Aaliyah admitted she had been more afraid of an awkward silence onstage than of getting the words wrong.\n\n'
   '8. Ms. Okoye did not restore the original apology line for opening night. She kept DeShawn\'s defiant version and Aaliyah\'s unscripted response, telling the cast that the accidental scene had revealed something about both characters that the original script had been too tidy to show.'))],
 [('What explicit line has Marcus said the same way for six weeks, according to paragraph 1?', '"I know I was wrong, and I\'m ready to face it."'),
  ('What specific incident happens in paragraph 2 that breaks from six weeks of rehearsal?', 'DeShawn, in character as Marcus, stops before his scripted apology and instead says, "I\'m not sorry. I\'d do it again, and you know why."'),
  ('What decision does DeShawn\'s line force onto Aaliyah in paragraph 3?', 'She must decide, in the length of one held breath, whether to break character to fix his mistake or stay in the scene and respond within it.'),
  ('How does Aaliyah\'s line in paragraph 4, "Then I guess we\'re done pretending you\'re the good guy," propel the action of the scene?', 'It is an unscripted line that immediately leads her to step between Marcus and the exit instead of letting the blocked exit happen, physically changing the direction the scene was supposed to take.'),
  ('What does Aaliyah\'s choice to stay in character reveal about her as a performer, based on paragraph 4?', 'It reveals quick thinking and composure under pressure — rather than freezing or breaking the scene, she immediately generates a plausible in-character response.'),
  ('How does DeShawn\'s incident with the prop chair in paragraph 5 further propel the action beyond his original off-script line?', 'It escalates the improvised conflict physically, turning what began as one unexpected line into an ongoing, unscripted confrontation that the rest of the cast must now react to.'),
  ('What does DeShawn\'s new line in paragraph 5, "Nobody made you the judge," reveal about Marcus\'s character in this improvised version of the scene?', 'It reveals a more defiant, less repentant version of Marcus than the script originally showed, someone who resents being confronted rather than someone ready to apologize.'),
  ('What specific incident in paragraph 6 shows Ms. Okoye making a real-time decision about the unscripted scene?', 'She does not call "cut" even though her hand hovers near the signal flashlight, and she lets the scene run four more unscripted lines before intervening.'),
  ('How does Ms. Okoye\'s choice not to stop the scene immediately (paragraph 6) affect what happens next in the story?', 'It allows the improvised confrontation to continue long enough to become something Ms. Okoye later decides is worth keeping, rather than being cut short as a simple mistake.'),
  ('What explicit reason does DeShawn give in paragraph 7 for going off-script?', 'He had been carrying real frustration from an argument with his older brother that afternoon, and it "came out sideways" during the scene.'),
  ('What does DeShawn\'s explanation in paragraph 7 reveal about the relationship between an actor\'s real life and their performance?', 'It reveals that an actor\'s outside emotions can unintentionally shape a performance, showing that DeShawn\'s defiant improvisation was not a calculated acting choice but an unplanned result of his real frustration.'),
  ('What does Aaliyah\'s admission in paragraph 7 reveal about her real motivation during the scene?', 'It reveals her in-character response was driven partly by a practical fear of dead air onstage, not purely by inspired acting instinct, adding a more ordinary and honest motive to her quick thinking.'),
  ('What decision does Ms. Okoye make in paragraph 8, and how was it provoked by the earlier incident?', 'She decides to keep DeShawn\'s defiant version and Aaliyah\'s unscripted response for opening night; this decision was directly provoked by witnessing how the accidental scene revealed something about both characters that the original script had not.'),
  ('Identify one line of dialogue in the story that does more than one job (reveals character AND propels action), and explain both effects.', '"I\'m not sorry. I\'d do it again, and you know why" (paragraph 2) reveals a more defiant version of Marcus\'s character while also propelling the action by forcing Aaliyah into an immediate, unplanned decision about how to respond.'),
  ('How would the story\'s ending be different if Aaliyah had chosen to break character in paragraph 3 instead of staying in the scene?', 'The improvised confrontation would likely have stopped immediately, the original scripted apology might have simply been restarted, and Ms. Okoye would have had no unscripted material to consider keeping for opening night.'),
  ('Explain how the incident in paragraphs 2 through 5 provokes Ms. Okoye\'s decision in paragraph 8, citing at least two specific moments.', 'DeShawn\'s unscripted line in paragraph 2 and Aaliyah\'s improvised response in paragraph 4 together create an unplanned but compelling version of the scene; by letting it continue in paragraph 6 rather than stopping it, Ms. Okoye gathers enough material to decide in paragraph 8 that the improvised version reveals more about the characters than the original script did.'),
  ('What explicit detail in paragraph 6 shows the rest of the cast was as surprised as the audience by DeShawn\'s change?', 'The rest of the cast froze, unsure whether to follow the printed blocking or whatever was happening in front of them, showing they had no advance warning either.'),
  ('How does the incident of DeShawn grabbing the prop chair in paragraph 5 provoke a specific decision from Ms. Okoye described in paragraph 6?', 'It is one of the escalating unscripted moments that leads Ms. Okoye to keep her hand hovering near the stop signal rather than using it immediately, showing the incident is actively provoking her real-time decision about whether to intervene.'),
  ('Compare the incident that reveals character in paragraph 2 (DeShawn\'s line) to the incident that reveals character in paragraph 7 (his explanation). What does each reveal, and how are the revelations different?', 'Paragraph 2\'s incident reveals character indirectly, through Marcus\'s defiant in-scene words; paragraph 7\'s incident reveals DeShawn\'s real character directly, through his own honest, out-of-character explanation — one shows character through performance, the other through DeShawn speaking as himself.'),
  ('If Ms. Okoye had restored the original scripted apology line for opening night instead of keeping the improvised version, how would that change what the ending reveals about Marcus\'s character?', 'It would return to showing Marcus as straightforwardly repentant, losing the more complicated, defiant version of the character that the unscripted incident had revealed, and the story would no longer show how an unplanned incident can improve on a script.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.3
add('ri83-connections-distinctions', 'Two Towns, One Flood', 'RI.8.3',
 'Analyze how a text makes connections among and distinctions between individuals, ideas, or events (e.g., through comparisons, analogies, or categories).',
 'Analyze how an informational text draws connections and distinctions between individuals, ideas, or events, including through direct comparisons, analogies, or categories.',
 'At grade 8, this standard asks you to notice the specific method a text uses to relate or separate two or more things — comparison (showing similarity), distinction (showing difference), analogy (explaining one thing through a similar but different thing), or category (grouping things by a shared trait). Identify the method, not just that a connection exists.',
 [('A text says, "Both towns received the same amount of rainfall, but only one flooded." What method is being used, and what is it setting up?',
   'This is a comparison that sets up a contrast — since the shared cause (rainfall) was identical, the text is preparing to explain some other difference between the towns that explains the different outcome.'),
  ('A text explains a new policy by saying, "Think of it like a dam: it doesn\'t stop the water, it just controls when it\'s released." What method is this, and why might a writer choose it?',
   'This is an analogy, which explains an unfamiliar idea (the policy) through a more familiar comparison (a dam); a writer might choose it to make an abstract policy easier to picture concretely.')],
 [('Two Towns, One Flood (Part 1)', (
   '1. In April 2022, six inches of rain fell over two neighboring river towns, Ashford and Millport, in under eighteen hours — nearly identical totals recorded at weather stations nine miles apart. Ashford\'s river crested two feet above flood stage. Millport\'s crested four inches below it. The rainfall was the same; the outcomes were not.\n\n'
   '2. The two towns share a similar size, about 8,000 residents each, and both sit along the same river system, separated by a single upstream dam built in the 1950s. Ashford lies downstream of the dam; Millport lies upstream. That single geographic distinction — which side of the dam each town sits on — turned out to explain far more than the rainfall totals did.\n\n'
   '3. Millport\'s upstream position meant its excess water drained directly into the reservoir behind the dam, which engineers had deliberately kept three feet below capacity that spring specifically to absorb an unusually wet forecast. Ashford, downstream, received whatever the dam operators chose to release, on a schedule set by reservoir safety limits, not by Ashford\'s immediate needs.\n\n'
   '4. A hydrologist interviewed after the flood compared the dam\'s operation to a kitchen sink with only one working faucet: "Upstream is like turning the tap — you control how much comes out and when. Downstream, you\'re just the drain, and the drain doesn\'t get a vote."')),
  ('Two Towns, One Flood (Part 2)', (
   '5. That analogy captures a real distinction in the two towns\' options. Millport\'s town council could and did request the dam operators delay a scheduled release by six hours, buying time for its own storm drains to catch up. Ashford\'s council had no equivalent lever; the release schedule downstream was set entirely by the dam operators\' calculations, not by any request Ashford could make.\n\n'
   '6. Researchers studying the flood later grouped river towns nationally into two categories based on this same upstream-downstream distinction, finding that downstream towns experienced 60 percent more flood damage per capita than upstream towns along regulated rivers, even when rainfall totals were statistically similar.\n\n'
   '7. The comparison between Ashford and Millport also revealed a connection the two towns share despite their different outcomes: both had underfunded their storm-drain systems by similar amounts over the previous decade, a shared factor that worsened flooding in both towns even though it did not, by itself, explain why only one town crossed flood stage.\n\n'
   '8. After the flood, Ashford\'s council proposed a formal notification agreement with the dam operators, modeled explicitly on Millport\'s existing arrangement — an attempt to close the very distinction between the two towns that the flood had exposed.'))],
 [('What explicit rainfall comparison does paragraph 1 make between Ashford and Millport?', 'Both towns received nearly identical rainfall totals, about six inches in under eighteen hours, recorded at weather stations nine miles apart.'),
  ('What method does paragraph 1 use when it says "The rainfall was the same; the outcomes were not"? What is this method setting up?', 'This is a direct comparison used to create a contrast; it sets up the article\'s explanation that some factor other than rainfall must explain the different outcomes.'),
  ('What explicit geographic distinction does paragraph 2 identify between the two towns?', 'Ashford lies downstream of the dam, and Millport lies upstream of it.'),
  ('How does paragraph 2 connect the towns before distinguishing them? Name both the connection and the distinction.', 'The connection is that the towns are similar in size (about 8,000 residents each) and sit along the same river system; the distinction is their position relative to the dam, upstream versus downstream.'),
  ('What explicit detail in paragraph 3 explains why Millport\'s upstream position helped during the flood?', 'The reservoir behind the dam had deliberately been kept three feet below capacity that spring to absorb an unusually wet forecast, giving Millport\'s excess water somewhere to go.'),
  ('What category of control does paragraph 3 say Ashford lacked, compared to Millport?', 'Ashford had no control over water release timing; releases were set by the dam operators based on reservoir safety limits, not by Ashford\'s needs.'),
  ('Identify the analogy used in paragraph 4 and explain what two things it compares.', 'The hydrologist compares the dam system to a kitchen sink with one working faucet, comparing the upstream town\'s control (turning the tap) to the downstream town\'s lack of control (being "just the drain").'),
  ('Why might the hydrologist have chosen a kitchen sink analogy instead of simply restating the engineering facts from paragraph 3?', 'A kitchen sink is a familiar, everyday image that makes an unfamiliar engineering relationship (upstream versus downstream water control) easier for a general reader to picture and understand quickly.'),
  ('What explicit action does paragraph 5 (Part 2) describe Millport\'s council taking that Ashford\'s council could not?', 'Millport\'s council requested and received a six-hour delay in a scheduled dam release, buying time for its storm drains to catch up.'),
  ('What category do researchers create in paragraph 6, and what distinction does it track nationally?', 'Researchers grouped river towns nationally into upstream and downstream categories along regulated rivers, tracking the distinction in flood damage per capita between the two categories.'),
  ('What explicit statistic supports the researchers\' categorization in that paragraph?', 'Downstream towns experienced 60 percent more flood damage per capita than upstream towns, even when rainfall totals were statistically similar.'),
  ('What connection does paragraph 7 identify between Ashford and Millport that goes against the upstream/downstream distinction?', 'Both towns had similarly underfunded their storm-drain systems over the previous decade, a shared weakness despite their different flood outcomes.'),
  ('Why is the connection in paragraph 7 important for a complete understanding of the flood, even though it does not explain the difference in outcomes?', 'It shows the flood was not caused by a single distinction alone; a shared, unrelated weakness (underfunded storm drains) worsened conditions in both towns even as the upstream/downstream distinction explains why only one town crossed flood stage.'),
  ('What explicit action does Ashford\'s council take in paragraph 8, and what distinction is it specifically trying to close?', 'Ashford\'s council proposes a formal notification agreement with the dam operators, modeled on Millport\'s existing arrangement, trying to close the upstream/downstream control distinction identified earlier in the article.'),
  ('Explain how the article uses comparison, distinction, and analogy together across paragraphs 1 through 4 to build its explanation.', 'The article first uses comparison to show the rainfall was the same (paragraph 1), then identifies the key distinction of upstream versus downstream position (paragraph 2), then uses an analogy (the kitchen sink, paragraph 4) to make that engineering distinction easy to picture, building from data to explanation to a relatable image.'),
  ('Which is a stronger piece of evidence for the article\'s explanation: the kitchen sink analogy or the 60 percent damage statistic? Justify your answer.', 'The 60 percent damage statistic is stronger evidence, because it is a measured, verifiable finding across many towns, while the analogy is only an explanatory device that makes the idea easier to understand and proves nothing on its own.'),
  ('A reader claims the flood happened only because Ashford is downstream. Using evidence from the article, explain why this oversimplifies the article\'s explanation.', 'This oversimplifies it because paragraph 7 shows both towns shared an unrelated weakness, underfunded storm drains, that worsened flooding in both places; the upstream/downstream distinction explains the difference in outcome, but it is not the article\'s only identified cause.'),
  ('Compare how paragraph 2 and paragraph 8 both use the idea of distinction, but for different purposes.', 'Paragraph 2 uses distinction to explain why the flood outcomes differed, while paragraph 8 uses the same distinction to justify a proposed solution, showing the article moving from diagnosing a problem to addressing it, using the same core distinction both times.'),
  ('Explain how the article\'s use of categories (paragraph 6) strengthens the individual comparison between Ashford and Millport made earlier in the article.', 'By showing that the upstream/downstream damage pattern holds true nationally across many towns, not just Ashford and Millport, the categorization strengthens the article\'s claim that the two towns\' different outcomes were not a coincidence specific to them, but part of a broader, measurable pattern.'),
  ('Why does the article wait until paragraph 8 to describe Ashford\'s proposed solution, rather than introducing it earlier alongside the initial comparison in paragraph 1?', 'The article needs to first establish the distinction (upstream/downstream), the analogy explaining it, the national statistic confirming it, and the shared storm-drain connection before a proposed solution would make sense to the reader; introducing it earlier would come before the article has built the evidence to justify it.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.4
add('rl84-word-choice-tone', 'What the Order Didn\'t Say', 'RL.8.4',
 'Determine the meaning of words and phrases as they are used in a text, including figurative and connotative meanings; analyze the impact of specific word choices on meaning and tone, including analogies or allusions to other texts.',
 'Determine figurative and connotative meanings of words and phrases in a literary text, and analyze how specific word choices shape meaning and tone.',
 'Denotation is a word\'s dictionary meaning; connotation is the feeling or association it carries beyond that meaning. Figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification) means something beyond its literal words. At grade 8, comparing word choices across two texts about the same event is a powerful way to see how word choice alone can create very different tones.',
 [('Compare the connotation of "the family relocated" versus "the family fled." Both can describe the same event — how do the connotations differ?',
   '"Relocated" carries a calm, planned, even bureaucratic connotation, suggesting control and choice, while "fled" carries urgency and fear, suggesting the family had little choice and left in danger.'),
  ('In the phrase "the smoke crawled over the ridge like something that knew where it was going," what figurative device is used, and what tone does it create?',
   'This is a simile (and personification, since smoke is given intention), and it creates an ominous, threatening tone by making the smoke seem deliberate and predatory rather than simply a natural weather event.')],
 [('Evacuation Order 14-B', (
   'RIDGELINE COUNTY OFFICE OF EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT\nEVACUATION ORDER 14-B\nEffective immediately, all residents of Zones 3 through 6 are directed to relocate to designated shelters. Residents should gather essential documents and medications and proceed to Route 9 southbound. Non-compliance may result in restricted emergency service access. This order remains in effect until further notice.')),
  ('What the Order Didn\'t Say', (
   '1. The order came through Priya\'s phone at 6:14 a.m., a flat gray banner with no exclamation points, no color, nothing to suggest it was the reason her mother was already dragging the fireproof document box down the stairs two at a time. "Relocate to designated shelters." As if the house were a piece of furniture they were simply moving to a different room.\n\n'
   '2. Outside, the sky had turned a color Priya had never had a word for before that morning — not orange exactly, more like the inside of a bruise, and the smoke crawled over the ridge like something that knew where it was going. The order had called it "reduced visibility due to active conditions." Priya thought that was like calling a wildfire "a warm afternoon."\n\n'
   '3. Her little brother, Theo, wanted to know if their house counted as "essential documents." Their mother said no, gently, the kind of gentle that Priya recognized as the sound of someone keeping a much louder feeling folded up small. Theo asked again anyway, holding his stuffed rabbit like it might answer for him.\n\n'
   '4. They joined Route 9 southbound along with what felt like the entire ridge, headlights strung out ahead of them like a sentence with no punctuation, just comma after comma of red taillights with no end in sight. The order had said "proceed." It had not said anything about the woman two cars ahead who kept getting out to look back at the smoke, or the way the radio DJ\'s voice cracked once, just once, before he caught it and went back to reciting the shelter addresses in his steady announcer voice.\n\n'
   '5. "Restricted emergency service access," the order had warned, if they did not comply. Priya turned the phrase over and over. It sounded like a rule about a parking lot, not a sentence about whether help would come if they stayed. She understood, suddenly and completely, that the order had been written by someone whose job was to be understood by everyone, and that being understood by everyone sometimes meant sounding like it mattered to no one.\n\n'
   '6. At the shelter, a volunteer handed them water bottles and said, "You\'re safe now," and that phrase, unlike anything in the order, was the first thing all morning that had sounded like it was written for Priya specifically, and not for Zones 3 through 6.'))],
 [('What explicit phrase does the official order use to describe leaving one\'s home, according to paragraph 1?', '"Relocate to designated shelters."'),
  ('What is the connotation of "relocate" as Priya interprets it in paragraph 1, and how does that differ from what is actually happening?', 'Priya interprets "relocate" as sounding calm and administrative, like moving furniture to a different room, when the actual event is an urgent, frightening evacuation from danger.'),
  ('Identify the simile in paragraph 2 describing the smoke, and explain what it suggests about the smoke\'s behavior.', '"The smoke crawled over the ridge like something that knew where it was going" is a simile that suggests intention and menace, as if the smoke were a living thing deliberately advancing rather than simply spreading with the wind.'),
  ('How does Priya\'s comparison in paragraph 2 ("calling a wildfire \'a warm afternoon\'") reveal her opinion of the order\'s word choice?', 'It reveals she thinks the order\'s phrase "reduced visibility due to active conditions" dangerously understates the danger, comparing it to an equally absurd understatement of a wildfire as pleasant weather.'),
  ('What does the phrase "the kind of gentle that Priya recognized as the sound of someone keeping a much louder feeling folded up small" suggest about their mother\'s emotional state?', 'It suggests their mother is suppressing fear or panic beneath a calm exterior for Theo\'s sake, and that her gentleness is a controlled performance rather than an absence of strong feeling.'),
  ('Identify the figurative comparison in paragraph 4 describing the traffic, and explain what it conveys about the scene.', 'The headlights are compared to "a sentence with no punctuation, just comma after comma of red taillights with no end in sight," conveying an overwhelming, unending line of evacuating cars with no clear resolution in sight.'),
  ('What two specific incidents in paragraph 4 does the narrator note that the official order\'s language never captures?', 'A woman two cars ahead repeatedly getting out to look back at the smoke, and a radio DJ\'s voice cracking once before he composes himself and continues reciting shelter addresses.'),
  ('What is the denotative meaning of "restricted emergency service access," and what connotation does Priya sense underneath it in paragraph 5?', 'Denotatively, it means emergency responders may not be able to reach non-compliant residents; Priya senses underneath it the real, frightening possibility that help might not come at all, though the phrase itself sounds bureaucratic and mild.'),
  ('Why does Priya compare the phrase "restricted emergency service access" to "a rule about a parking lot"?', 'She makes this comparison to highlight how mismatched the phrase\'s mild, procedural tone is to the life-or-death situation it actually describes.'),
  ('What insight does Priya reach in paragraph 5 about why the order is written the way it is?', 'She realizes the order was written to be understood by everyone equally, and that this kind of universal, neutral language can end up sounding like it matters to no one in particular, even though it is meant for everyone.'),
  ('How does the volunteer\'s phrase "You\'re safe now" in paragraph 6 contrast in tone with the official order\'s language throughout the story?', 'Unlike the order\'s flat, procedural phrases, the volunteer\'s words are warm and personal, addressed to Priya specifically rather than to an entire numbered zone, creating a tone of individual care instead of bureaucratic instruction.'),
  ('Why does the narrator specifically note that the volunteer\'s phrase felt "written for Priya specifically, and not for Zones 3 through 6"?', 'It emphasizes the story\'s larger point about word choice and tone: official, general language can technically communicate the same information as warm, specific language, but it does not make a listener feel seen or cared for the way personal language does.'),
  ('Compare the tone of the official order text to the tone of Priya\'s narrative. Use one word-choice example from each to support your comparison.', 'The order\'s tone is flat and procedural, shown by phrases like "proceed to Route 9 southbound"; Priya\'s narrative is vivid and emotional, shown by figurative language like comparing the smoke to something that "knew where it was going."'),
  ('Choose one phrase from the official order and explain how its word choice avoids alarming readers, even though the situation is dangerous.', '"Reduced visibility due to active conditions" avoids words like "fire," "danger," or "smoke," using vague, technical-sounding language that describes a hazardous situation without naming it directly, which likely reduces panic but also reduces urgency.'),
  ('If the order had instead said "a dangerous wildfire is approaching your home," how might the tone and effect on residents be different from the actual order\'s wording?', 'It would create a more urgent, alarming tone that clearly names the danger, which might prompt faster compliance from residents but could also risk causing panic that the order\'s calmer, more procedural language was likely designed to avoid.'),
  ('What is the denotative meaning of "reduced visibility due to active conditions," and what does Priya believe it is designed to avoid saying directly?', 'Denotatively, it means smoke is making it hard to see, caused by the fire\'s ongoing spread; Priya believes it is designed to avoid saying "wildfire" or "fire" directly, softening the danger through vague, technical phrasing.'),
  ('Identify the figurative language in "a color Priya had never had a word for before that morning — not orange exactly, more like the inside of a bruise," and explain what feeling it conveys.', 'The comparison of the sky to "the inside of a bruise" is a simile that conveys a sickly, painful, unnatural feeling, suggesting the sky itself looks injured or wrong.'),
  ('Why might the author have chosen to include the official order as a separate, first text before Priya\'s narrative, rather than only telling the story from Priya\'s point of view?', 'Presenting the order\'s flat, procedural language first lets readers directly compare its tone to Priya\'s vivid, emotional narrative that follows, making the contrast in word choice and tone much clearer than if only one version were given.'),
  ('Choose one word from the official order and explain both its technical/denotative meaning and the connotation it might carry for a frightened resident.', 'Answers vary; for example, "designated" technically just means officially assigned or specified, but to a frightened resident it might carry a connotation of cold, impersonal bureaucracy at a moment of personal crisis.'),
  ('Trace how the story\'s tone shifts from paragraph 1 to paragraph 6, citing specific word choices at the beginning and end.', 'The story begins with a flat, confused tone as Priya reads the bureaucratic order ("relocate to designated shelters," paragraph 1) and ends with a warmer, more personal tone at the shelter, when the volunteer\'s words "You\'re safe now" (paragraph 6) finally sound, in Priya\'s words, "written for Priya specifically."')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.4
add('ri84-technical-word-choice', 'Words in the White Coat', 'RI.8.4',
 'Determine the meaning of words and phrases as they are used in a text, including figurative, connotative, and technical meanings; analyze the impact of specific word choices on meaning and tone, including analogies or allusions to other texts.',
 'Determine figurative, connotative, and technical meanings of words and phrases in an informational text, and analyze how specific word choices shape meaning and tone.',
 'Informational texts often mix technical meanings (precise, field-specific definitions) with connotative meanings (the feelings a word carries). At grade 8, notice when a writer chooses a more clinical, technical word versus a more everyday or emotionally loaded one, and consider what effect that choice has on the reader.',
 [('A medical report could describe a patient as "obese" or as "carrying extra weight." What is the difference between these two word choices?',
   '"Obese" is a precise technical/clinical term with a specific medical definition, while "carrying extra weight" is a softer, more everyday phrase; the technical term sounds more clinical and less personal, while the everyday phrase may feel gentler but less precise.'),
  ('If a doctor says a treatment has a "statistically significant effect," what technical meaning does "significant" have here that differs from its everyday connotation of "important" or "large"?',
   'In statistics, "significant" technically means the result is unlikely to be due to random chance, which is a specific mathematical claim; it does not necessarily mean the effect is large or clinically important, which is how the word is often used in everyday speech.')],
 [('Words in the White Coat: How Doctors Choose Language for Patients (Part 1)', (
   '1. When Dr. Elena Morrow was a medical resident, a senior physician told her something she still repeats to her own students two decades later: "You have exactly one chance to choose the first word a patient hears about their diagnosis, and that word will color everything they hear after it." The word, in that case, was "mass." Not "tumor," not "growth," not "cancer" — just "mass," a technically accurate but deliberately neutral term used until further testing could clarify what kind of mass it was.\n\n'
   '2. Medical language operates on at least two levels at once: the technical level, where a word has a precise, field-specific definition regardless of how it sounds, and the connotative level, where the same word carries emotional weight a patient will feel immediately. "Tumor" is technically neutral — it simply means an abnormal mass of tissue, which can be benign or malignant — but its everyday connotation is almost entirely frightening, often assumed to mean cancer even when it does not.\n\n'
   '3. This gap between technical meaning and connotation creates real dilemmas. A 2021 survey of oncologists found that 68 percent said they deliberately chose less alarming words during an initial diagnosis conversation, even when a more clinical term was technically more precise, specifically to avoid overwhelming a patient before they were ready to absorb full information.')),
  ('Words in the White Coat: How Doctors Choose Language for Patients (Part 2)', (
   '4. Word choice matters even in written reports patients read themselves. Dr. Morrow points to the shift many hospitals have made from "obese" to "BMI above 30" or "elevated BMI" in visit summaries. The number is more technical and, according to Morrow, "somehow feels less like a judgment and more like a measurement," even though both phrases describe the identical clinical finding.\n\n'
   '5. Some word choices rely on analogy rather than technical precision. Dr. Morrow often describes chemotherapy to patients as "clearing a garden that has both the plant you want and the weeds choking it out — the tools cannot always tell the difference." She avoids the still-common military analogy of "fighting" or "battling" cancer, which she says can unintentionally suggest a patient who dies from the disease somehow "lost," as if survival were purely a matter of effort.\n\n'
   '6. Technical terms are not always more comforting than emotional ones. When Dr. Morrow tells a patient a treatment showed a "statistically significant" improvement, some patients hear "significant" and assume a dramatic, life-changing result, when the technical meaning only confirms the result was unlikely to be due to chance and says nothing about its actual size.\n\n'
   '7. Morrow\'s current guidance to her residents is not to avoid technical language altogether, but to name the gap out loud: to say, in effect, "here is the precise medical term, and here is what it does and does not mean for you," treating word choice itself as part of patient care rather than a formality that happens before the real conversation begins.'))],
 [('What explicit word does paragraph 1 identify as Dr. Morrow\'s first choice when a diagnosis is still uncertain?', '"Mass."'),
  ('What technical reason does paragraph 1 give for choosing "mass" instead of more specific terms?', 'It is technically accurate but deliberately neutral, used until further testing can clarify what kind of mass it actually is.'),
  ('Explain the distinction paragraph 2 draws between a word\'s technical meaning and its connotative meaning, using "tumor" as the example.', 'Technically, "tumor" simply means an abnormal mass of tissue that can be benign or malignant; connotatively, most patients hear the word and immediately assume cancer, even though that is not what the technical definition requires.'),
  ('What explicit statistic does paragraph 3 provide about oncologists\' word choices during initial diagnosis conversations?', '68 percent of oncologists surveyed in 2021 said they deliberately chose less alarming words, even when a more clinical term was technically more precise.'),
  ('Why, according to paragraph 3, might a doctor choose a less precise word over a more technically accurate one?', 'To avoid overwhelming a patient with alarming language before the patient is ready to absorb full information about their condition.'),
  ('What explicit shift in terminology does paragraph 4 describe hospitals making, and what technical meaning does the new term have?', 'A shift from "obese" to "BMI above 30" or "elevated BMI," where BMI is a specific, calculated numerical measurement rather than a descriptive label.'),
  ('According to Dr. Morrow\'s quote in paragraph 4, what is the connotative effect of using a number ("BMI above 30") instead of a descriptive word ("obese")?', 'It "feels less like a judgment and more like a measurement," even though both phrases technically describe the same clinical finding.'),
  ('Identify the analogy Dr. Morrow uses to describe chemotherapy in paragraph 5, and explain what two things it compares.', 'She compares chemotherapy to "clearing a garden that has both the plant you want and the weeds choking it out," comparing healthy and cancerous cells to desired plants and weeds, and the imprecision of treatment to a gardening tool that cannot always tell them apart.'),
  ('Why does Dr. Morrow avoid the "fighting" or "battling" analogy for cancer, according to paragraph 5?', 'She says it can unintentionally suggest that a patient who dies from the disease somehow "lost," implying survival is purely a matter of effort, which she considers an unfair and inaccurate connotation.'),
  ('What is the technical meaning of "statistically significant," as explained in paragraph 6?', 'It technically means the result is unlikely to be due to random chance; it says nothing about how large or dramatic the actual effect is.'),
  ('How does the everyday connotation of "significant" in paragraph 6 differ from its technical statistical meaning, and why does this matter for patients?', 'Everyday "significant" suggests something large or dramatic, while the technical meaning only confirms the result is not due to chance; this matters because patients may wrongly expect a dramatic improvement based on the everyday connotation alone.'),
  ('What is Dr. Morrow\'s current guidance to her residents, according to paragraph 7?', 'Not to avoid technical language, but to explicitly name the gap between the technical term and what it does and does not mean for the patient.'),
  ('Why does the article end with Dr. Morrow\'s guidance in paragraph 7 rather than with the chemotherapy analogy in paragraph 5? What does this suggest about the article\'s central point?', 'Ending with her guidance suggests the article\'s central point is not just that word choice matters, but that doctors should be deliberate and transparent about it, treating the choice of words as an active, ongoing part of patient care rather than a single clever word or analogy.'),
  ('Compare the tone created by "mass" (paragraph 1) to the tone created by "tumor" (paragraph 2), even though both can technically describe the same thing.', '"Mass" creates a calm, neutral, even slightly vague tone, buying time before a clearer picture emerges, while "tumor" creates an alarmed tone because of its strong connotative association with cancer, even when the underlying finding is identical.'),
  ('A reader claims doctors should always use the most technically precise word possible, regardless of connotation. Using evidence from the article, explain why Dr. Morrow\'s practice complicates this claim.', 'Dr. Morrow deliberately avoids the most alarming precise term in initial conversations (paragraph 1) and studies show most oncologists do the same (paragraph 3), showing that in practice, doctors weigh a word\'s emotional impact alongside its technical precision rather than always choosing the most exact term available.'),
  ('What is the technical, field-specific meaning of "BMI," and how does paragraph 4 suggest the number format changes the tone of a clinical finding compared to a descriptive word?', 'BMI (body mass index) is a specific numerical measurement calculated from height and weight; paragraph 4 suggests presenting it as a number feels more neutral and measurement-like, while a descriptive word like "obese" can feel more like a personal judgment, even though both describe the same finding.'),
  ('Explain the figurative comparison in paragraph 5 between chemotherapy and gardening, identifying what each part of the analogy represents.', 'The "plant you want" represents healthy cells, the "weeds choking it out" represent cancerous cells, and "the tools cannot always tell the difference" represents chemotherapy\'s inability to perfectly distinguish healthy from cancerous cells, explaining a complex medical limitation through a familiar, everyday image.'),
  ('What connotation does the "fighting" or "battling" analogy for cancer carry, according to Dr. Morrow in paragraph 5, and why does she consider it potentially unfair?', 'It carries a connotation that surviving is a matter of effort or willpower, like winning a fight; she considers it unfair because it implies a patient who dies somehow "lost" or did not try hard enough, when survival often depends on factors beyond a patient\'s control.'),
  ('Compare the connotative effect of the gardening analogy in paragraph 5 to the connotative effect of the "fighting" analogy she avoids. Why might one feel gentler than the other?', 'The gardening analogy focuses on the imprecision of a tool doing careful work, which feels gentler and more forgiving, while the fighting analogy frames the patient as personally responsible for winning or losing, which can feel more like praise or blame directed at the patient.'),
  ('Explain how the article\'s title, "Words in the White Coat," uses connotation to introduce its subject before the reader reaches paragraph 1.', 'The "white coat" is a strong visual and cultural symbol of medical authority and expertise, so pairing it with "words" in the title immediately signals that the article is specifically about how that authority is expressed and shaped through language choices, not just medical facts themselves.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.5
add('rl85-structure-shapes-meaning', 'Match Point, Told Two Ways', 'RL.8.5',
 'Compare and contrast the structure of two or more texts and analyze how the differing structure of each text contributes to its meaning and style.',
 'Compare the structure of two literary texts describing the same event, and analyze how each text\'s structure shapes its meaning and style.',
 'Structure is the order and arrangement of events in a text, not the events themselves. Chronological structure moves start to finish in time order. Other structures — starting near the end and flashing back, or jumping between moments — can create suspense, emphasize a specific moment, or change what the reader focuses on, even when telling the exact same story.',
 [('A story about a car accident could be told start-to-finish, or it could open with the ambulance arriving and then flash back to what led up to it. What effect does opening with the ambulance create that starting from the beginning would not?',
   'Opening with the ambulance immediately signals to the reader that something serious happens, creating tension and curiosity about how the characters got there, an effect a purely chronological version would build toward more slowly.'),
  ('If two texts describe the same graduation ceremony, but one is organized chronologically and the other is organized by "before/during/after" reflections from one character, how might their styles differ?',
   'The chronological version would likely read as a straightforward narrative account, while the reflective version would likely feel more personal and introspective, focused on the character\'s emotional experience rather than the sequence of events itself.')],
 [('Match Point (Told in Order)', (
   '1. Deja had lost the first two games of the match to Vikram, the top seed at the regional chess tournament, and by the third game she was down a rook with no clear plan to get it back. She reset her water bottle cap three times before making her next move, a habit her coach called "stalling with style."\n\n'
   '2. On move nineteen, Vikram offered a pawn trade that looked harmless. Deja stared at the board for four full minutes, longer than she had spent on any move that day, before declining it and repositioning her knight instead.\n\n'
   '3. Vikram\'s eyebrows rose slightly, the first visible reaction he had shown all match. He had expected the trade to be accepted automatically; declining it meant Deja had seen something in the position he had not expected her to see.\n\n'
   '4. Seven moves later, Deja\'s repositioned knight forked Vikram\'s queen and rook simultaneously. Vikram sat back in his chair, exhaled once, and extended his hand across the board before Deja could even reach for her own piece. "Good game," he said. "I missed that four moves ago."')),
  ('Match Point (Told Backward)', (
   '1. Vikram extended his hand across the board, resigning before Deja could even move her fork-threatening knight into position to capture. "Good game," he said. "I missed that four moves ago." Deja shook his hand, still not quite believing the position in front of her.\n\n'
   '2. Four moves earlier, that same knight had been sitting on an ordinary square, part of a quiet repositioning that Vikram\'s raised eyebrows suggested he had not expected. He had offered a pawn trade minutes before, certain Deja would accept it automatically, the way most players at her level did against him.\n\n'
   '3. She had not accepted it. She had stared at the board for four full minutes, the longest she had spent on any single move all day, turning over a possibility that would not become obvious to Vikram until it was already too late to stop.\n\n'
   '4. All of it — the four-minute pause, the declined trade, the eventual fork — traced back to a third game that had started with Deja down a rook and no clear plan, water bottle cap clicking three times as she searched for one.'))],
 [('What explicit detail does paragraph 1 of the chronological version give about Deja\'s standing in the match before move nineteen?', 'She had lost the first two games and was down a rook in the third game, with no clear plan to recover it.'),
  ('In the chronological version, what specific incident happens on move nineteen (paragraph 2)?', 'Vikram offers a pawn trade that looks harmless, and Deja stares at the board for four minutes before declining it and repositioning her knight.'),
  ('What is the very first sentence of the backward-told version, and how does its content compare to the very last sentence of the chronological version?', 'The backward version opens with Vikram resigning and saying "Good game. I missed that four moves ago," which is nearly identical to the ending of the chronological version — the backward version starts where the other one ends.'),
  ('How does starting with the resignation in the backward version immediately change what the reader is curious about, compared to the chronological version?', 'Instead of wondering how the match will end, the reader immediately knows Deja wins and instead becomes curious about how she got there — the mystery shifts from outcome to cause.'),
  ('Identify one sentence that appears in both versions with nearly identical wording, and explain where each version places it.', '"Good game. I missed that four moves ago" appears at the very end of the chronological version and at the very beginning of the backward version, marking the same moment but serving opposite structural purposes: a climax versus an opening hook.'),
  ('How does the backward version\'s paragraph 2 use the phrase "that same knight" to link back to paragraph 1?', 'It explicitly connects the knight involved in the winning fork (paragraph 1) to an earlier, seemingly ordinary moment (paragraph 2), guiding the reader backward through cause and effect rather than forward through time.'),
  ('What information is revealed in paragraph 4 of the chronological version that is instead revealed in paragraph 4 of the backward version — but described as an origin rather than a starting point?', 'Deja being down a rook early in the third game with no clear plan; the chronological version starts there, while the backward version treats it as the root cause revealed only at the very end.'),
  ('Compare how each version treats the four-minute pause over move nineteen. What role does it play structurally in each version?', 'In the chronological version, the pause is presented in the moment as it happens, building suspense forward; in the backward version, the same pause is revealed partway through as an already-completed event that the reader now understands the significance of, building understanding backward.'),
  ('Which version\'s structure makes Vikram\'s reaction ("Good game. I missed that four moves ago.") feel more like a surprising reveal, and why?', 'The backward version makes it feel more like a reveal, because the reader encounters this line before understanding the sequence of moves that caused it, creating curiosity that the rest of the text then satisfies.'),
  ('Which version\'s structure makes Deja\'s four-minute pause over the declined trade feel more suspenseful in the moment it happens, and why?', 'The chronological version makes the pause feel more suspenseful in the moment, because the reader experiences it in real time without knowing yet whether Deja\'s decision will succeed, unlike the backward version where the outcome is already known.'),
  ('How does the chronological structure affect the story\'s style — is it more focused on building tension toward an unknown outcome, or explaining a known outcome? Support your answer.', 'It is more focused on building tension toward an unknown outcome; each paragraph moves forward in time without revealing the result, so the reader experiences uncertainty alongside Deja until the final move.'),
  ('How does the backward structure affect the story\'s style — is it more focused on building tension, or explaining causes? Support your answer.', 'It is more focused on explaining causes; since the outcome is revealed immediately, each subsequent paragraph functions like an investigation working backward to explain how that outcome came to be.'),
  ('If a reader wanted to feel maximum suspense about whether Deja would win, which version should they read, and why?', 'The chronological version, because it withholds the outcome until the final paragraph, allowing suspense to build naturally as the match unfolds.'),
  ('If a reader already knew Deja won and wanted to understand exactly how, which version would likely be more satisfying, and why?', 'The backward version, because it is structured specifically to trace the causes of a known outcome, walking the reader through exactly which decisions led to the win.'),
  ('Both versions contain the same four key incidents (the rook deficit, the declined trade, the four-minute pause, the knight fork). Explain how differing structure changes their meaning even though the events themselves do not change.', 'Answers vary; should explain that in the chronological version the incidents build toward an uncertain outcome, creating suspense, while in the backward version the same incidents are revealed as an explanation for an already-known outcome, creating a sense of discovery or realization instead — the structure changes what the reader is meant to feel and focus on, even though no event itself changes.'),
  ('What explicit detail does paragraph 3 of the backward version add about the declined trade that paragraph 2 of the chronological version does not directly state?', 'It explicitly notes the pause was "the longest she had spent on any single move all day," adding a comparative detail about the pause\'s length relative to the rest of her match that highlights its significance in hindsight.'),
  ('How does the phrase "traced back to" in paragraph 4 of the backward version signal the text\'s overall structural approach?', 'It explicitly signals that the text is working backward from an effect to its cause, guiding the reader to understand the final paragraph as an origin point rather than a conclusion.'),
  ('Which version\'s final paragraph functions as a true ending, and which version\'s final paragraph functions as a true beginning? Explain using structure, not content.', 'The chronological version\'s final paragraph is a true ending, wrapping up the sequence of events; the backward version\'s final paragraph is structurally a true beginning, since it describes the earliest event in time even though it appears last in the text.'),
  ('If you were writing a version of this story to emphasize Vikram\'s perspective and surprise, which structure would you choose, and why?', 'Answers vary; a strong answer might choose the backward structure, since opening with his resignation and surprise reaction immediately centers his emotional experience, or the chronological structure, arguing that showing his growing confusion in real time (the raised eyebrows, the earlier expectation) builds his perspective more gradually.'),
  ('Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of the backward structure compared to the chronological structure for a reader encountering this story for the first time.', 'An advantage is immediate intrigue, since starting with the resignation raises questions the reader wants answered; a disadvantage is reduced suspense about the outcome itself, since the reader already knows Deja wins before learning any of the details of how.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.5
add('ri85-paragraph-structure', 'The Magnetic Map: How Sea Turtles Navigate Home', 'RI.8.5',
 'Analyze in detail the structure of a specific paragraph in a text, including the role of particular sentences in developing and refining a key concept.',
 'Analyze in detail how a specific paragraph in an informational text is structured, identifying the role each sentence plays in developing and refining a key concept.',
 'Every well-built paragraph has sentences that do different jobs: a topic sentence that introduces the key concept, supporting sentences that provide evidence or explanation, a sentence that refines or complicates the idea, and sometimes a transition sentence that connects to what follows. At grade 8, identify not just what a paragraph says, but what job each individual sentence is doing.',
 [('In a paragraph, the first sentence states, "Coral reefs face a growing threat from ocean acidification." What job is this sentence most likely doing?',
   'It is most likely the topic sentence, introducing the paragraph\'s key concept (ocean acidification as a threat to coral reefs) that the rest of the paragraph will develop.'),
  ('If a later sentence in that same paragraph says, "However, some coral species have shown surprising resilience in early trials," what job is that sentence doing relative to the topic sentence?',
   'It is refining or complicating the topic sentence\'s claim, adding nuance by showing the threat is not uniform across all coral species, rather than simply repeating or supporting the original claim.')],
 [('The Magnetic Map: How Sea Turtles Navigate Home (Part 1)', (
   '1. Every year, loggerhead sea turtles hatch on beaches along the southeastern United States, swim thousands of miles across the open Atlantic, and eventually return to nest on the very same stretch of coastline where they were born, sometimes after more than a decade at sea. For years, scientists had a name for this ability but not a full explanation: natal homing.\n\n'
   '2. Researchers now believe the answer lies in a sense most humans do not have: magnetoreception, the ability to detect Earth\'s magnetic field. Every location on Earth has a slightly different magnetic signature, based on the angle and strength of the planet\'s magnetic field at that point. A sea turtle hatchling appears to imprint on the specific magnetic signature of its home beach within its first few weeks of life, the way some animals imprint visually on a parent.\n\n'
   '3. That imprinted signature becomes something like an internal map. As the turtle travels thousands of miles over years, it can sense whether it is north, south, east, or west of its home signature, adjusting its swimming direction accordingly, the same way a hiker might use a compass without needing to see the destination directly.')),
  ('The Magnetic Map: How Sea Turtles Navigate Home (Part 2)', (
   '4. A 2015 study tested this idea directly. Researchers exposed hatchling loggerheads to magnetic fields matching two different real locations along the Atlantic coast, then observed which direction the hatchlings tried to swim. The hatchlings consistently oriented themselves as if trying to move toward the coastline associated with their actual birth location, even though they had never experienced that magnetic field difference through any other sense.\n\n'
   '5. The theory is not without complications. Magnetic signatures drift slightly over time due to natural shifts in Earth\'s magnetic field, meaning a turtle\'s internal map, formed once in infancy, could theoretically become slightly mismatched with its home beach\'s current signature decades later. Some researchers propose this drift may explain rare cases of turtles nesting on beaches near, but not exactly at, their birth location.\n\n'
   '6. Despite these open questions, magnetoreception remains the leading explanation for one of the animal kingdom\'s most precise long-distance navigation feats, turning an ordinary beach into something closer to a magnetic address memorized before a turtle takes its first swim.'))],
 [('What explicit fact does paragraph 1 state about the distance loggerhead turtles travel before returning to nest?', 'They swim thousands of miles across the open Atlantic, sometimes returning after more than a decade at sea.'),
  ('What is the topic sentence of paragraph 2, and what key concept does it introduce?', 'The topic sentence is "Researchers now believe the answer lies in a sense most humans do not have: magnetoreception," which introduces magnetoreception as the key concept the rest of the paragraph will explain.'),
  ('What job does the second sentence of paragraph 2 ("Every location on Earth has a slightly different magnetic signature...") perform relative to the topic sentence?', 'It is a supporting sentence that provides background explanation needed to understand the topic sentence\'s claim, defining what a "magnetic signature" is before the paragraph can explain how turtles use it.'),
  ('What job does the third sentence of paragraph 2 ("A sea turtle hatchling appears to imprint...") perform?', 'It is a supporting sentence that applies the general concept of magnetic signatures specifically to sea turtles, explaining how and when the imprinting process happens.'),
  ('What is the topic sentence of paragraph 3, and how does it build directly on paragraph 2\'s final idea?', '"That imprinted signature becomes something like an internal map" is the topic sentence; it builds directly on paragraph 2\'s idea of imprinting by explaining what the imprinted signature is then used for.'),
  ('What job does the comparison to a hiker\'s compass, at the end of paragraph 3, perform in the paragraph\'s structure?', 'It is a clarifying/supporting sentence that uses a familiar analogy to make the abstract concept of magnetic navigation easier to understand, refining the reader\'s understanding of the internal map concept.'),
  ('What is the topic sentence of paragraph 4, and what evidence does the rest of the paragraph provide to support it?', '"A 2015 study tested this idea directly" is the topic sentence; the rest of the paragraph provides the study\'s method (exposing hatchlings to different magnetic fields) and its result (hatchlings oriented toward their birth location) as supporting evidence.'),
  ('Which sentence in paragraph 4 states the study\'s specific result, and how does it connect back to the magnetoreception concept from paragraph 2?', '"The hatchlings consistently oriented themselves as if trying to move toward the coastline associated with their actual birth location" states the result, directly confirming the magnetoreception and imprinting concepts introduced earlier in the article.'),
  ('What job does the first sentence of paragraph 5 ("The theory is not without complications.") perform in the paragraph\'s structure?', 'It is a refining/complicating sentence that signals a shift from supporting evidence to acknowledging limitations, preparing the reader for a more nuanced view of the theory.'),
  ('What specific complication does paragraph 5 introduce, and which sentence explains its possible real-world effect?', 'The complication is that magnetic signatures drift over time; the sentence "Some researchers propose this drift may explain rare cases of turtles nesting on beaches near, but not exactly at, their birth location" explains its possible real-world effect.'),
  ('How does the first sentence of paragraph 6 ("Despite these open questions...") function as a transition, and what two ideas does it connect?', 'It connects the complications raised in paragraph 5 back to the overall strength of the magnetoreception theory, acknowledging uncertainty while reaffirming the theory\'s status as the leading explanation.'),
  ('What job does the article\'s final phrase, comparing a beach to "a magnetic address memorized before a turtle takes its first swim," perform?', 'It is a concluding sentence that restates the article\'s key concept in a memorable, figurative way, giving the reader a final, vivid image that ties together imprinting, magnetic signatures, and navigation.'),
  ('Choose paragraph 2 and explain, sentence by sentence, how each sentence builds toward a fuller understanding of magnetoreception.', 'Sentence 1 introduces magnetoreception as the key concept; sentence 2 explains the background fact (magnetic signatures vary by location) needed to understand it; sentence 3 explains specifically how sea turtles use this ability (imprinting on their home signature), moving from general concept to specific mechanism across the three sentences.'),
  ('Why might the article place the 2015 study\'s evidence (paragraph 4) after the explanation of magnetoreception (paragraphs 2–3) rather than before it?', 'Placing the explanation first gives the reader the background needed to understand why the study\'s results are significant; presenting the evidence first, without that context, would make the study\'s findings harder to interpret.'),
  ('Explain how paragraph 5\'s structure (introducing a complication, then explaining its possible effect) differs from paragraph 2\'s structure (introducing a concept, then explaining it directly). What is the purpose of each structure?', 'Paragraph 2 is structured to build understanding of a new concept step by step; paragraph 5 is structured to introduce doubt and then explain its implications, serving a different purpose — testing and qualifying the theory rather than simply explaining it.'),
  ('If paragraph 5 (the complications) were removed entirely, how would that change the overall structure and credibility of the article?', 'Removing paragraph 5 would make the article read as more one-sided and fully settled, losing the added credibility that comes from acknowledging open questions and showing the theory is still being tested and refined by researchers.'),
  ('What explicit fact does paragraph 1 state about how scientists previously understood natal homing?', 'They had a name for the ability, "natal homing," but not a full explanation for how it worked.'),
  ('How does the first sentence of paragraph 1 set up the concept that paragraph 2\'s topic sentence will answer?', 'Paragraph 1 ends by naming the unexplained phenomenon (natal homing) without a mechanism, and paragraph 2\'s topic sentence directly answers that gap by introducing magnetoreception as the likely explanation.'),
  ('What is the topic sentence of paragraph 6, and how does it function differently from the topic sentences of paragraphs 2 through 5?', '"Despite these open questions, magnetoreception remains the leading explanation..." is the topic sentence of paragraph 6; unlike the earlier topic sentences, which each introduce a new piece of the explanation or a complication, this one functions as a summary that reaffirms the overall theory after weighing its complications.'),
  ('Explain how the structure of paragraphs 4 and 5 together mirrors the structure of scientific reasoning: presenting evidence, then testing its limits.', 'Paragraph 4 presents supporting evidence for the theory (the 2015 study\'s results), and paragraph 5 immediately follows by testing the theory\'s limits (the complication of magnetic drift), mirroring how real scientific reasoning builds support for an idea and then honestly examines where that idea might not fully hold.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.6
add('rl86-dramatic-irony', 'What Naomi Already Knew', 'RL.8.6',
 'Analyze how differences in the points of view of the characters and the audience or reader (e.g., created through the use of dramatic irony) create such effects as suspense or humor.',
 'Analyze how a gap between what the reader knows and what a character knows creates dramatic irony, and explain the humor or suspense that gap produces.',
 'Dramatic irony happens when the reader or audience knows something a character does not. The size and nature of that knowledge gap determines the effect: if the reader worries about what will happen when the truth comes out, dramatic irony creates suspense; if the mismatch itself is amusing, it creates humor.',
 [('In a story, the reader knows a character\'s "surprise" party has already been ruined by a leaked group chat, but the party planners do not know the guest of honor knows. What effect does this gap most likely create?',
   'It most likely creates humor, since the reader can anticipate the planners\' earnest, oblivious effort colliding with the guest of honor\'s pretending to be surprised, which is an amusing mismatch rather than a dangerous one.'),
  ('In a different story, the reader knows a character is walking toward a meeting where they will be fired, but the character believes they are being promoted. What effect does this gap most likely create?',
   'It most likely creates suspense or dramatic tension, since the reader anticipates a painful, consequential realization, making the character\'s confident anticipation uncomfortable to read rather than funny.')],
 [('What Naomi Already Knew (Part 1)', (
   '1. Marcus had planned Naomi\'s surprise party down to the minute: 6:45, guests arrive and hide; 6:58, Naomi\'s mom texts the "we\'re five minutes away" signal; 7:00, lights off; 7:02, Naomi walks in the door and everyone yells "surprise." He had rehearsed his own line, "Wait, what — you thought I forgot your birthday?", four times in the mirror that morning.\n\n'
   '2. What Marcus did not know was that Naomi had been added to the party-planning group chat by accident two weeks earlier, when her cousin Deion mistyped a phone number by one digit. Naomi had read all one hundred and forty-three messages, including the argument about whether to get a bounce house (no) and Marcus\'s four separate drafts of his "surprise" line.\n\n'
   '3. Naomi had told no one that she knew. Instead, she had spent the two weeks planning something of her own: a fake, elaborately bad birthday outfit — mismatched shoes, a shirt on inside out, hair deliberately disheveled — designed to make it look, when she "discovered" the surprise, like she had genuinely been caught completely off guard on her worst-dressed day.\n\n'
   '4. At 6:58, her mother\'s "we\'re five minutes away" text arrived exactly on schedule. Naomi, sitting in the passenger seat in her deliberately disastrous outfit, felt her phone buzz and had to bite the inside of her cheek to keep from laughing at the message: "ETA 5 min, everyone in position, DO NOT respond to this text Naomi might see your phone light up."')),
  ('What Naomi Already Knew (Part 2)', (
   '5. Marcus, hiding behind the couch with eleven other classmates, whispered the countdown under his breath: three, two, one. The lights were off. Someone\'s elbow was uncomfortably close to someone else\'s ear. He could hear his own heartbeat, certain this was going to be the best surprise anyone at school had ever pulled off.\n\n'
   '6. The door opened. Naomi stepped in, took one look at the dark, silent living room, and — right on cue — let her face go slack with theatrical, wide-eyed shock a half second before the lights snapped on and thirty voices screamed "SURPRISE!"\n\n'
   '7. She staggered back a step, hand pressed to her chest, and said, in a voice just a little too polished to be spontaneous, "Wait — is this — Marcus, did you plan this?" Marcus beamed, utterly convinced, and delivered his rehearsed line right on cue: "Wait, what — you thought I forgot your birthday?"\n\n'
   '8. It was not until three weeks later, cleaning out her phone\'s message drafts, that Naomi accidentally forwarded Deion the wrong screenshot — one of her own texts from the day of the party, timestamped 6:59, that read: "putting on the worst outfit I own, wish me luck." Deion, confused, showed it to Marcus. Marcus read it twice before he understood, and then he laughed so hard he had to sit down.'))],
 [('What explicit plan does paragraph 1 describe Marcus rehearsing?', 'His line for when Naomi walks in: "Wait, what — you thought I forgot your birthday?", rehearsed four times in the mirror.'),
  ('What does the reader learn in paragraph 2 that Marcus does not know throughout the entire party?', 'That Naomi was accidentally added to the party-planning group chat two weeks earlier and has read all 143 messages, including Marcus\'s rehearsed line.'),
  ('At what point does the reader gain more knowledge than Marcus has? Identify the specific paragraph and explain the gap it creates.', 'Paragraph 2 creates the gap; the reader learns Naomi already knows about the party, while Marcus continues believing, through paragraphs 5 through 7, that the surprise is genuine.'),
  ('What explicit plan does Naomi make in paragraph 3 once she knows about the party?', 'She plans a fake, elaborately bad birthday outfit designed to make her reaction look like genuine surprise on her "worst-dressed day."'),
  ('How does paragraph 4\'s detail about Naomi biting her cheek "to keep from laughing" depend on the reader already knowing what Marcus does not?', 'It is only funny to the reader because they know, as Naomi does, that the "secret" text is not actually secret to her; without that earlier knowledge from paragraph 2, her reaction would not make sense.'),
  ('What does paragraph 5 reveal about Marcus\'s emotional state right before the surprise, and how does the reader\'s knowledge from paragraph 2 change how we read it?', 'Marcus feels genuine excitement and certainty that this will be "the best surprise anyone at school had ever pulled off"; because the reader already knows the surprise is not a surprise to Naomi, his confidence creates dramatic irony rather than simple excitement.'),
  ('What explicit performance does Naomi give in paragraph 6, and what word in the text signals to the reader that it is not real?', 'She lets her face go slack with "theatrical, wide-eyed shock," and the word "theatrical" explicitly signals to the reader that her reaction is a deliberate performance, not genuine surprise.'),
  ('Identify the detail in paragraph 7 that most clearly reveals Naomi\'s reaction is staged, and explain why it is effective without directly stating "she was acting."', 'The narrator describes her voice as "just a little too polished to be spontaneous," which lets the reader infer she is acting without stating it outright, reinforcing the dramatic irony already established.'),
  ('How does the identical repetition of Marcus\'s rehearsed line, from paragraph 1 to paragraph 7, contribute to the story\'s humor?', 'The reader already knows from paragraph 1 that Marcus rehearsed this exact line, so hearing him deliver it "right on cue" in paragraph 7, believing it to be spontaneous, is funny precisely because the reader knows how planned and unoriginal it actually is.'),
  ('Where does the story\'s dramatic irony resolve — that is, when does a character other than Naomi finally learn what the reader has known all along?', 'It resolves in paragraph 8, when Marcus accidentally sees Naomi\'s old text and realizes she knew about the party the whole time.'),
  ('How does the resolution in paragraph 8 shift the story\'s dramatic irony instead of ending it? Who is now in on the secret, and who might not be?', 'Instead of ending the irony, it shifts who holds the knowledge — now Marcus and the reader share full knowledge, while the rest of the party guests may still not know Naomi was never actually surprised, so a new, smaller irony gap remains.'),
  ('Compare the tone of paragraphs 5 through 7 (before Marcus learns the truth) to paragraph 8 (after he learns it). How does the dramatic irony shape each tone?', 'Paragraphs 5–7 have a warm, earnest tone built on Marcus\'s sincere excitement, which the reader enjoys partly because they know something he does not; paragraph 8 shifts to a comedic tone once the gap closes and Marcus himself can finally appreciate the joke.'),
  ('If the story had been told entirely from Marcus\'s point of view, without ever revealing Naomi\'s group-chat mishap to the reader, how would the story\'s effect change?', 'Without paragraph 2\'s reveal, the story would read as a simple, sincere surprise-party account with no dramatic irony; the humor specifically depends on the reader knowing what Marcus does not, so hiding that information from the reader as well would eliminate the humor entirely.'),
  ('Explain, using at least two specific examples from the text, how this story uses dramatic irony to create humor rather than suspense.', 'The gap is used for humor rather than suspense because the mismatched knowledge is low-stakes and amusing rather than dangerous — for example, Naomi biting her cheek to avoid laughing at a "secret" text she already knows about (paragraph 4), and Marcus proudly delivering a rehearsed line he believes is spontaneous (paragraph 7) — both moments make the reader smile at the mismatch rather than worry about it.'),
  ('What explicit detail in paragraph 2 explains exactly how Naomi ended up in the group chat?', 'Her cousin Deion mistyped a phone number by one digit, accidentally adding her to the party-planning group chat two weeks earlier.'),
  ('Why is it significant that Naomi "told no one that she knew" (paragraph 3), for the dramatic irony that follows?', 'It ensures the knowledge gap stays entirely between Naomi and the reader; if she had told even one other character, the irony would involve more people and would likely be discovered before the party itself.'),
  ('What specific physical details does paragraph 3 give about Naomi\'s planned "disastrous" outfit?', 'Mismatched shoes, a shirt worn inside out, and hair deliberately disheveled.'),
  ('How does knowing about Naomi\'s planned outfit (paragraph 3) change how a reader interprets her appearance when she arrives at the party in paragraph 6?', 'Without that knowledge, a reader might assume Naomi simply looked disheveled from a busy day; knowing it was deliberately planned turns her appearance into part of the joke, reinforcing that her whole "surprised" persona was staged in advance.'),
  ('What single word does the narrator use in paragraph 6 that most directly tells the reader Naomi\'s shock is not genuine?', '"Theatrical."'),
  ('Describe the moment where the reader\'s knowledge and Marcus\'s knowledge are furthest apart. Why is that specific moment the peak of the story\'s dramatic irony?', 'The peak is paragraph 7, when Marcus delivers his rehearsed line "utterly convinced" while Naomi performs equally convincing fake surprise; at that exact moment, the reader alone knows both characters are performing for each other, making it the point of maximum gap between what the reader and the characters understand.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.6
add('ri86-authors-purpose-counterevidence', 'The Case for a Later Bell', 'RI.8.6',
 'Determine an author\'s point of view or purpose in a text and analyze how the author acknowledges and responds to conflicting evidence or viewpoints.',
 'Determine an author\'s point of view or purpose in an informational or persuasive text, and analyze how the author acknowledges and responds to conflicting evidence or opposing viewpoints.',
 'An author\'s purpose is what they want the text to accomplish (to inform, persuade, or both); their point of view is the position they hold on the topic. Strong persuasive writing at grade 8 often directly acknowledges opposing evidence or viewpoints rather than ignoring them, then explains why the author\'s position still holds despite that opposing evidence.',
 [('An article arguing for a new bike lane states: "Some worry a bike lane will slow car traffic. Traffic studies from three similar cities found delays of under sixty seconds during peak hours." What is the author doing here?',
   'The author is acknowledging a specific opposing concern (slower traffic) and then directly responding to it with evidence, rather than ignoring the concern or pretending it does not exist.'),
  ('If that same article never mentioned traffic concerns at all, only listing reasons bike lanes are beneficial, how would that change a reader\'s trust in the argument?',
   'A reader might trust the argument less, since ignoring an obvious counterargument can make a persuasive text seem one-sided or unaware of real objections, rather than confident enough to address them directly.')],
 [('The Case for a Later Bell (Part 1)', (
   '1. Our school day begins at 7:25 a.m., which means most students wake by 6:00 to be ready in time. Sleep researchers have found that adolescent brains naturally shift toward later sleep and wake cycles during the teenage years, a biological change, not a matter of discipline or bedtime habits. Starting school this early, for this age group, works against biology rather than with it.\n\n'
   '2. The evidence for a later start time is substantial. A 2019 study across eight districts that delayed start times by at least thirty minutes found an 11 percent drop in reported daytime sleepiness among students and a measurable improvement in first-period attendance. Districts that shifted to a 8:30 a.m. or later start also reported fewer car accidents among teen drivers, likely connected to reduced drowsy driving during early morning commutes.\n\n'
   '3. I recognize this proposal is not free of real complications, and I want to address the two objections I hear most often directly, rather than pretend they do not exist.')),
  ('The Case for a Later Bell (Part 2)', (
   '4. The first objection is athletics and after-school activities. Coaches and parents worry that a later school day pushes practices and games later into the evening, cutting into time for homework, family dinners, and sleep — the very thing this proposal is meant to protect. This is a legitimate concern. Districts that delayed start times successfully did not simply add thirty minutes to the end of the day; several shifted athletic practice earlier by making corresponding adjustments to bus schedules and shortening the school day\'s midday block, keeping the overall school-plus-activities day roughly the same length.\n\n'
   '5. The second objection is transportation cost. Many districts run two staggered bus routes, with elementary schools starting later than secondary schools specifically to reuse the same buses and drivers. Reversing that order, so secondary schools start later instead, could require additional buses or drivers, a real cost that smaller districts in particular have cited as a barrier. Some districts have addressed this by staggering elementary and secondary start times only slightly differently rather than reversing them entirely, reducing but not eliminating the added cost.\n\n'
   '6. Neither objection is imaginary, and neither has been fully solved everywhere a later start time has been proposed. But in districts that found workable adjustments to both athletics scheduling and transportation costs, the sleep and safety benefits held, suggesting the objections are practical obstacles to plan around, not reasons the underlying case for a later start time is wrong.'))],
 [('What explicit claim does paragraph 1 make about adolescent sleep cycles?', 'Adolescent brains naturally shift toward later sleep and wake cycles during the teenage years, a biological change rather than a matter of discipline or habit.'),
  ('What is the author\'s stated purpose in this article, and where is it most clearly expressed?', 'The author\'s purpose is to persuade readers to support a later school start time; it is most clearly expressed in the title and in paragraph 1\'s claim that early start times "work against biology rather than with it."'),
  ('What explicit statistic does paragraph 2 provide about the 2019 study\'s findings on daytime sleepiness?', 'An 11 percent drop in reported daytime sleepiness among students in districts that delayed start times by at least thirty minutes.'),
  ('What does the author explicitly state their intention is in paragraph 3?', 'To directly address the two objections heard most often, rather than pretend they do not exist.'),
  ('What is the first objection the author acknowledges in paragraph 4, and who does the author say raises it?', 'The objection that a later school day pushes athletics and after-school activities later into the evening; the author says coaches and parents raise this concern.'),
  ('How does the author respond to the athletics objection in paragraph 4, rather than simply dismissing it?', 'The author cites specific evidence that some districts adjusted bus schedules and shortened the school day\'s midday block, keeping the overall school-plus-activities day roughly the same length, directly addressing the concern with a practical solution rather than denying it matters.'),
  ('What is the second objection the author acknowledges in paragraph 5, and what is its specific cause?', 'The objection of increased transportation cost, caused by many districts reusing the same buses and drivers for staggered elementary and secondary routes, which reversing would disrupt.'),
  ('How does the author\'s response to the transportation objection in paragraph 5 differ in confidence from the response to the athletics objection in paragraph 4?', 'The transportation response is more measured, admitting some districts have only "reduced but not eliminated the added cost," while the athletics response describes districts that kept the day\'s overall length "roughly the same," suggesting a more complete solution was found for that objection.'),
  ('What explicit statement does the author make in paragraph 6 about whether either objection has been fully solved?', 'The author explicitly states that neither objection is imaginary and neither has been fully solved everywhere a later start time has been proposed.'),
  ('Why might the author choose to admit that the objections are not fully solved everywhere, rather than claiming they have been completely resolved?', 'Admitting the objections are not fully solved makes the author appear honest and credible rather than overselling the proposal, which can make the overall argument more persuasive to a skeptical reader.'),
  ('How does the phrase "practical obstacles to plan around, not reasons the underlying case... is wrong" in paragraph 6 summarize the author\'s overall response to both objections?', 'It reframes both objections as solvable logistical problems rather than fundamental flaws in the argument, allowing the author to acknowledge real difficulties while still maintaining their original point of view.'),
  ('Identify the author\'s point of view on later school start times, and cite one piece of evidence used to support it.', 'The author supports later school start times; one piece of supporting evidence is the 2019 study\'s finding of an 11 percent drop in daytime sleepiness across eight districts.'),
  ('Explain how the structure of this article (claim, then evidence, then two objections and responses) helps achieve the author\'s persuasive purpose.', 'This structure builds the case with evidence first, establishing credibility, and then proactively addresses likely objections, which can make skeptical readers feel their concerns are being taken seriously rather than ignored, strengthening the overall persuasive effect.'),
  ('A reader claims the author only mentions objections to make the article seem fair, without really taking them seriously. Using evidence from paragraphs 4 through 6, evaluate whether this claim is accurate.', 'This claim is not well supported; paragraphs 4 and 5 include specific evidence of how some districts practically addressed each objection, and paragraph 6 explicitly admits neither objection is fully solved everywhere, showing the author engages with the objections substantively rather than dismissing them for appearance only.'),
  ('If the article had ended after paragraph 2, without addressing any objections, how might that change a skeptical reader\'s response to the author\'s point of view?', 'A skeptical reader might find the argument less convincing or one-sided, since real logistical concerns like athletics and transportation would go unaddressed, potentially making the proposal seem impractical or naive about real-world implementation challenges.'),
  ('What explicit detail does paragraph 2 give about the connection between later start times and teen driver safety?', 'Districts that shifted to an 8:30 a.m. or later start reported fewer car accidents among teen drivers, likely connected to reduced drowsy driving during early morning commutes.'),
  ('Why does the author include the driver-safety statistic in paragraph 2 in addition to the sleepiness statistic? What does it add to the argument?', 'It broadens the argument beyond comfort or academic performance to a concrete public-safety concern, giving the author\'s point of view an additional, more urgent kind of supporting evidence.'),
  ('What transition does paragraph 3 provide between the evidence-focused paragraphs (1–2) and the objection-focused paragraphs (4–5)?', 'Paragraph 3 explicitly announces the shift, stating the author will address the two most common objections directly rather than pretend they do not exist, signaling a change in the article\'s structure and purpose.'),
  ('Compare the phrase "a real cost that smaller districts in particular have cited as a barrier" (paragraph 5) to the phrase "this is a legitimate concern" (paragraph 4). What do both phrases reveal about the author\'s approach to opposing viewpoints?', 'Both phrases show the author validating the opposing viewpoint as genuine and reasonable rather than exaggerated or dismissed, which is consistent with the article\'s overall strategy of acknowledging objections honestly before responding to them.'),
  ('Explain how the author\'s point of view in this article differs from simply presenting "both sides equally." Use evidence to support your answer.', 'The author clearly favors later start times throughout, presenting supporting evidence first and using most of the article to argue for the position; the objections are acknowledged and responded to, but the article does not treat the case for and against later start times as equally weighted, since it consistently returns to evidence supporting the author\'s original point of view.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.7
add('rl87-script-vs-production', 'The Director\'s Choices', 'RL.8.7',
 'Analyze the extent to which a filmed or live production of a story or drama stays faithful to or departs from the text or script, evaluating the choices made by the director or actors.',
 'Compare a script excerpt to a description of its staged production, and evaluate the extent to which specific directorial and acting choices stay faithful to or depart from the written text.',
 'A script is a blueprint; a production is one specific interpretation of it. Directors and actors constantly make choices — about pacing, tone, staging, even cutting or adding lines — that are not required by the script itself. At grade 8, evaluate whether a specific choice serves the script\'s original meaning, changes it in an interesting way, or works against it.',
 [('A script\'s stage direction simply says, "She exits." If a director has the actor exit slowly, looking back twice, is that faithful to the script or a departure from it?',
   'It is a departure in specific detail (the script does not require looking back or a slow pace), but it could still be faithful to the script\'s meaning if the added detail fits the character\'s established emotional state — departures in staging detail are not automatically unfaithful to the text\'s meaning.'),
  ('If a script includes a joke that a director cuts entirely from the final production, what should an evaluation of that choice consider?',
   'It should consider why the joke might have been cut (pacing, tone mismatch, timing) and whether removing it changes the scene\'s meaning or the audience\'s understanding of a character, not simply whether a line was removed.')],
 [('Scene 12, "The Interview" (Original Script)', (
   'INT. OFFICE — DAY\n\nMARGUERITE sits across from her interviewer, hands folded.\n\nINTERVIEWER: Tell me about a time you failed.\n\nMARGUERITE: (pause) I don\'t know that I\'d call it failure.\n\nINTERVIEWER: Then what would you call it?\n\nMARGUERITE: A very expensive lesson.\n\n(She almost smiles. The INTERVIEWER writes something down. MARGUERITE watches him write, trying to read it upside down.)\n\nINTERVIEWER: That\'s all I needed to hear.\n\nMARGUERITE exits.')),
  ('Director\'s Notes on the Staged Production', (
   'In our production, we kept every line of Scene 12 exactly as written — no cuts, no additions to the dialogue. But we made three staging choices the script doesn\'t specify. First, we had a full fifteen seconds of silence before Marguerite answers "I don\'t know that I\'d call it failure," much longer than the single-word "(pause)" suggests, to let the audience feel her genuinely searching for an answer rather than performing confidence. Second, we cut the line "trying to read it upside down" from the stage direction — meaning the actor no longer visibly strains to see the interviewer\'s notes, and instead sits perfectly still, hands still folded, which our director felt made her seem more controlled and less anxious than the original direction implies. Third, we added a full turn back toward the interviewer just before Marguerite exits, a beat not in the script at all, so the audience sees one last flicker of doubt cross her face before she leaves.'))],
 [('What explicit dialogue line does Marguerite say in response to "Tell me about a time you failed"?', '"I don\'t know that I\'d call it failure."'),
  ('What explicit stage direction does the original script give for how Marguerite responds before her first line?', 'A single-word direction: "(pause)."'),
  ('What is the first staging choice the director\'s notes describe, and how does it depart from the script\'s original stage direction?', 'The director extended the single "(pause)" into a full fifteen seconds of silence, a much longer and more specific pause than the script\'s brief direction suggests.'),
  ('According to the director\'s notes, what effect was this extended pause meant to create in the audience?', 'It was meant to let the audience feel Marguerite genuinely searching for an answer, rather than performing confidence.'),
  ('What explicit detail from the original script\'s stage direction does the production choose to cut entirely?', 'The direction that Marguerite watches the interviewer write, "trying to read it upside down."'),
  ('How does cutting that detail change the character\'s portrayal, according to the director\'s notes?', 'Without that detail, the actor sits perfectly still with hands folded, which the director felt made Marguerite seem more controlled and less anxious than the original stage direction implies.'),
  ('Is the second staging choice (cutting the detail) faithful to the script\'s dialogue, its stage directions, or neither? Explain.', 'It is faithful to the script\'s dialogue, since no spoken lines are cut, but it is a departure from the script\'s stage directions, since a specific physical action described in the text is removed entirely.'),
  ('What explicit addition does the production make just before Marguerite exits, according to the third staging choice?', 'A full turn back toward the interviewer, showing one last flicker of doubt on her face, a beat that does not appear anywhere in the original script.'),
  ('The original script simply says "MARGUERITE exits." Evaluate whether the added turn-back beat is faithful to or departs from this direction, and explain your reasoning.', 'It technically departs from the script, since "exits" alone specifies no additional action; however, it could still be considered faithful to the character\'s established uncertainty (shown through her hesitant first line), since the added doubt is consistent with, rather than contradictory to, who Marguerite seems to be in the written dialogue.'),
  ('Which of the three staging choices most changes an audience\'s understanding of Marguerite\'s emotional state, and why?', 'The second choice (cutting the "reading upside down" detail) most changes the audience\'s understanding, because it shifts Marguerite from someone anxiously trying to gather information to someone outwardly composed, altering a specific character trait rather than just adding emotional texture.'),
  ('Which of the three choices adds emotional texture without changing any specific fact about Marguerite\'s character as written?', 'The extended pause (the first choice) mainly adds emotional texture — a longer, more visible moment of searching — without changing any concrete fact the script establishes about her, since a pause of some length is already called for.'),
  ('Evaluate whether the director\'s choices, taken together, strengthen or weaken the original script\'s central idea about Marguerite reframing failure as "a very expensive lesson." Support your answer with at least two of the three choices.', 'The choices likely strengthen the script\'s central idea: the extended pause (choice one) makes her eventual composed answer feel more earned, and the added doubtful glance while exiting (choice three) suggests the reframing is not fully resolved for her internally, adding depth to the line rather than undercutting it.'),
  ('If a critic argued the production was "unfaithful" to the script simply because it made staging choices not written in the text, how would you respond using evidence from the director\'s notes?', 'This argument oversimplifies faithfulness; the production kept every line of dialogue exactly as written, and its staging choices, while not literally specified in the script, are used to support and deepen the same character and central idea the script establishes, rather than contradict them.'),
  ('Choose one staging choice and argue it works against, rather than supports, the script\'s original meaning. Use specific evidence.', 'Answers vary; a reasonable argument is that cutting the "reading upside down" detail (choice two) removes a moment of visible vulnerability the script specifically included, which could work against the script\'s apparent interest in showing Marguerite\'s anxiety beneath her composed answers.'),
  ('Write one sentence evaluating the overall production: does it stay mostly faithful to the script, or does it substantially depart from it? Support your evaluation with evidence from at least two staging choices.', 'Answers vary; require a clear evaluative claim (faithful, departing, or a mix) supported by specific reference to at least two of the three staging choices described in the director\'s notes.'),
  ('What explicit claim does the director\'s notes make about the dialogue at the very start of the notes, before describing any staging choices?', 'That every line of Scene 12 was kept exactly as written, with no cuts or additions to the dialogue itself.'),
  ('Why might the director have chosen to state upfront that the dialogue was kept intact, before listing the staging changes? What does this framing suggest about the production\'s intention?', 'It suggests the production wants to be understood as fundamentally faithful to the script\'s words, framing the staging choices as interpretation within that faithfulness rather than as rewriting the scene.'),
  ('What explicit word does Marguerite\'s line "A very expensive lesson" replace, based on the interviewer\'s earlier question about "failure"?', 'It reframes "failure" as a "lesson," showing Marguerite choosing not to accept the interviewer\'s word for her experience.'),
  ('How does the third staging choice (the added turn-back and flicker of doubt) relate to Marguerite\'s reframing of failure as a "lesson" in her dialogue? Does it support or complicate that reframing?', 'It likely complicates the reframing, suggesting that despite her composed, reframed answer in dialogue, some genuine doubt or unresolved feeling remains beneath the surface, adding complexity rather than simply supporting or undermining her stated position.'),
  ('A reader who has only read the script, not seen the production, would understand Marguerite very differently in at least one way. Identify that difference and explain which of the three staging choices causes it.', 'A reader of the script alone would likely assume Marguerite is anxiously trying to gather information, based on the "reading upside down" detail; a viewer of the production would instead see her as outwardly composed and controlled, because the second staging choice removes that detail entirely.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.7
add('ri87-medium-tradeoffs', 'A Shipwreck, Told Three Ways', 'RI.8.7',
 'Evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of using different mediums (e.g., print or digital text, video, multimedia) to present a particular topic or idea.',
 'Evaluate the specific advantages and disadvantages of presenting the same topic through different mediums, such as print text, video, and interactive multimedia.',
 'Every medium has built-in strengths and limitations that shape what it can communicate well. Print text is easy to skim and revisit at your own pace but cannot show motion or sound. Video can show action and emotion but forces every viewer through the same pace. Interactive multimedia lets users explore at their own pace but requires more effort and technology to use well.',
 [('A museum plaque about a historical event is limited to about 150 words. What is one advantage and one disadvantage of this print medium for telling the story?',
   'An advantage is that visitors can read it quickly, at their own pace, without needing any device; a disadvantage is that 150 words cannot capture the same depth, emotion, or complexity that a longer format like video or an in-depth article could.'),
  ('A documentary film about the same event can include survivor interviews with real emotion in their voices. What advantage does video have here that a printed pamphlet does not?',
   'Video can capture tone of voice, facial expression, and emotional nuance directly, giving the audience an immediate emotional connection that a written transcript of the same words could only describe secondhand.')],
 [('A Shipwreck, Told Three Ways (Part 1)', (
   '1. In 1898, the cargo ship Meridian Star sank off the coast of a small fishing town during an unexpected November storm, killing eleven of the eighteen crew members. Today, the story of the Meridian Star is told to visitors through three very different mediums: a bronze plaque at the harbor, a thirty-minute documentary film shown at the local museum, and an interactive website maintained by a maritime history nonprofit.\n\n'
   '2. The harbor plaque, installed in 1953, contains exactly 142 words: the ship\'s name, the date, the death toll, and two sentences describing the storm. Visitors passing by on a walk can read the entire plaque in under a minute, absorbing the basic facts without needing to seek out a museum, a film screening, or an internet connection. Its main limitation is depth — the plaque has no room for individual crew members\' stories, no explanation of why the ship was caught in the storm, and no way to convey what the disaster actually felt like to those aboard or waiting on shore.\n\n'
   '3. The documentary film, produced in 2004, dedicates its full thirty minutes to interviews with descendants of survivors and crew members who died. One interview, with the great-granddaughter of a surviving deckhand, includes a long pause and a visibly shaking voice as she describes a pocket watch that washed ashore three days after the wreck. That specific emotional weight, conveyed through tone of voice and a trembling pause, is something the plaque\'s 142 words could never carry, no matter how well written.')),
  ('A Shipwreck, Told Three Ways (Part 2)', (
   '4. But the documentary has its own limitations. Visitors must set aside a full thirty minutes and be physically present at a specific museum screening time, or seek out a copy elsewhere. Unlike the plaque, it cannot be skimmed for a single fact in ten seconds, and unlike a website, a viewer cannot jump directly to the one interview they are most interested in without fast-forwarding through the rest.\n\n'
   '5. The interactive website, launched in 2018, takes a third approach entirely: a zoomable map of the wreck site, clickable profiles of all eighteen crew members with individual biographies, and an interactive timeline visitors can drag through hour by hour on the night of the storm. A visitor specifically interested in one crew member\'s story can go directly to that profile in seconds, something neither the plaque nor the documentary allows.\n\n'
   '6. The website\'s disadvantages are just as real. It requires internet access and a device, immediately excluding visitors without either. It also demands more active effort from the user — clicking, exploring, deciding where to go next — compared to the plaque\'s effortless minute-long read or the documentary\'s guided, passive thirty minutes. A distracted or rushed visitor might explore only a fraction of the website\'s content, missing information that a linear documentary would have delivered to them regardless of their engagement level.\n\n'
   '7. No single medium tells the Meridian Star\'s story completely. The plaque reaches the widest, most casual audience; the documentary delivers emotional depth no text can match; the website offers control and detail no fixed-length format allows. Together, and only together, the three mediums cover what any one of them, alone, cannot.'))],
 [('What explicit facts does paragraph 1 provide about the Meridian Star disaster?', 'It sank in 1898 off the coast of a small fishing town during an unexpected November storm, killing eleven of the eighteen crew members.'),
  ('What explicit word count does paragraph 2 give for the harbor plaque, and what four pieces of information does it contain?', '142 words, containing the ship\'s name, the date, the death toll, and two sentences describing the storm.'),
  ('What advantage of the plaque does paragraph 2 identify, related to how visitors encounter it?', 'Visitors can read the entire plaque in under a minute while passing by, without needing to seek out a museum, film screening, or internet connection.'),
  ('What specific disadvantage of the plaque does paragraph 2 identify?', 'Its lack of depth — no room for individual crew members\' stories, no explanation of the storm\'s cause, and no way to convey the emotional experience of the disaster.'),
  ('What specific moment from the documentary does paragraph 3 describe in detail?', 'An interview with the great-granddaughter of a surviving deckhand, including a long pause and a shaking voice as she describes a pocket watch that washed ashore three days after the wreck.'),
  ('What advantage of video does paragraph 3 highlight, using that specific interview as evidence?', 'Video can convey emotional weight through tone of voice and a trembling pause, an effect the plaque\'s limited word count could never achieve no matter how well written.'),
  ('What explicit disadvantage of the documentary does paragraph 4 identify regarding time and access?', 'Visitors must set aside a full thirty minutes and be physically present at a specific screening time, or seek out a copy elsewhere.'),
  ('How does paragraph 4 compare the documentary\'s flexibility unfavorably to both the plaque and the website?', 'It states the documentary cannot be skimmed for a single fact in ten seconds like the plaque, and a viewer cannot jump directly to one interview without fast-forwarding, unlike the website\'s direct-access design.'),
  ('What three interactive features does paragraph 5 describe as part of the website?', 'A zoomable map of the wreck site, clickable profiles of all eighteen crew members with individual biographies, and a draggable hour-by-hour timeline of the night of the storm.'),
  ('What advantage of the website does paragraph 5 highlight that neither the plaque nor the documentary offers?', 'A visitor interested in one specific crew member\'s story can go directly to that profile in seconds, rather than reading unrelated information or sitting through an entire linear program.'),
  ('What explicit disadvantage of the website does paragraph 6 identify regarding access?', 'It requires internet access and a device, immediately excluding visitors who lack either.'),
  ('What second disadvantage of the website, related to user effort, does paragraph 6 describe, and how does it compare to the documentary?', 'It demands more active effort from the user (clicking, exploring, deciding where to go), compared to the documentary\'s guided, passive experience that delivers information regardless of how engaged the viewer is.'),
  ('According to paragraph 6, what specific risk does a distracted visitor face when using the website, that they would not face with the documentary?', 'They might explore only a fraction of the website\'s content and miss information, whereas a linear documentary delivers its full content to a viewer regardless of their engagement level.'),
  ('What is the article\'s overall conclusion in paragraph 7 about which medium is "best" for telling this story?', 'No single medium is best; each has distinct advantages the others lack, and only using all three together covers what any single medium alone cannot.'),
  ('Create a two-column comparison for the plaque and the website only: one advantage and one disadvantage for each, using evidence from the article.', 'Plaque — advantage: readable in under a minute with no device needed (paragraph 2); disadvantage: no room for individual stories or explanation (paragraph 2). Website — advantage: direct access to specific crew profiles in seconds (paragraph 5); disadvantage: requires internet access and a device, excluding some visitors (paragraph 6).'),
  ('If a museum had to choose only one medium to preserve and could afford to fund only one, which would you recommend based on the article\'s evidence, and why?', 'Answers vary; a strong answer will choose one medium and support it with specific advantages from the text while acknowledging the specific information or effect that would be lost, showing understanding that every medium involves real tradeoffs rather than a single "correct" choice.'),
  ('What explicit installation and production years does the article give for the plaque, the documentary, and the website?', 'The plaque was installed in 1953, the documentary was produced in 2004, and the website was launched in 2018.'),
  ('Why might the article include the specific year each medium was created? What does this detail suggest about how the story has been told over time?', 'It suggests the story has been retold across generations as new mediums became available, with each new format adding capabilities (like emotional depth or interactivity) that earlier available technology could not provide.'),
  ('Compare the kind of effort required from a visitor for the plaque versus the website, using specific evidence from the article.', 'The plaque requires almost no effort, readable in under a minute while walking by (paragraph 2); the website requires active effort, described as "clicking, exploring, deciding where to go next" (paragraph 6), a much more demanding form of engagement.'),
  ('Explain how the documentary occupies a middle position between the plaque and the website in terms of effort and control, using evidence from the article.', 'The documentary requires more time commitment than the plaque, since it takes a full thirty minutes rather than a quick read, but it requires less active decision-making than the website, since it is described as "guided, passive" (paragraph 6) rather than something the viewer must navigate themselves.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RL.8.9
add('rl89-modern-underdog-pattern', 'The Last Bakery on Cedar Street', 'RL.8.9',
 'Analyze how a modern work of fiction draws on themes, patterns of events, or character types from myths, traditional stories, or religious works such as the Bible, including describing how the material is rendered new.',
 'Analyze how a modern story draws on a traditional story pattern, such as the underdog-versus-giant pattern found in myths and traditional stories, and describe how the modern version renders that pattern new.',
 'Many traditional stories share recognizable patterns: an outmatched underdog facing an overwhelming opponent, a trickster outwitting someone more powerful, a flood that washes away an old world to make room for a new one. A modern story can draw on one of these patterns while "rendering it new" — changing the setting, the stakes, or the resolution so it no longer resembles a retelling, but still carries the shape and meaning of the original pattern.',
 [('The ancient story of David and Goliath features a young, poorly armed shepherd defeating a heavily armored giant using a slingshot and a single stone. What is the core pattern of this story, stripped of its specific details?',
   'The core pattern is an underdog, seemingly powerless against a vastly stronger opponent, winning through an unconventional method rather than matching the opponent\'s own strength.'),
  ('If a modern story used this same underdog pattern but replaced the giant with a corporation and the slingshot with a social media campaign, would it still be "drawing on" the original pattern? Explain.',
   'Yes — the specific details (giant, slingshot) have changed entirely, but the underlying pattern (an overmatched underdog defeating a powerful opponent through an unconventional, clever method rather than brute force) remains intact, which is what "drawing on" a pattern means.')],
 [('The Last Bakery on Cedar Street (Part 1)', (
   '1. Rosa Delgado had run Delgado\'s Bakery on Cedar Street for twenty-two years, long enough to have baked the wedding cakes of couples who now brought their own children in for Saturday morning pan dulce. When BreadGiant, a national chain with over four thousand locations, announced it was opening a store two blocks away, offering loaves at half her price, Rosa did the math on a napkin and did not like the number she got.\n\n'
   '2. BreadGiant\'s regional director, in a local news interview, called Rosa\'s bakery "a quaint transition period" the neighborhood would move past within a year. He was not being cruel, exactly — he seemed to genuinely believe it, the way someone believes water will eventually fill any container it is poured into, without malice, simply because that is what water does.\n\n'
   '3. Rosa did not have four thousand locations, a regional marketing budget, or the ability to sell bread below her own cost the way a company with warehouses full of capital could afford to, for a while, to starve out a competitor. What she had was twenty-two years of birthdays, funerals, and Tuesday mornings where she remembered exactly how a customer took their coffee.')),
  ('The Last Bakery on Cedar Street (Part 2)', (
   '4. Rosa did not lower her prices to compete, and she did not run advertisements. Instead, she started a Tuesday night bread-making class in the back of the shop, free for anyone who signed up, teaching neighborhood kids and retirees alike how to shape a proper baguette. She could not out-market BreadGiant\'s multi-million-dollar campaign, but she could put her hands directly into two dozen people\'s lives every week, one loaf at a time.\n\n'
   '5. Word of the classes spread the way small things spread in a neighborhood that still talked to itself: not through an algorithm, but through people telling other people. Six months in, a dozen former students had started baking their own bread at home instead of buying anyone\'s — including, unexpectedly, three of them who now volunteered to help Rosa on her busiest mornings, unpaid, simply because they wanted her shop to survive.\n\n'
   '6. BreadGiant\'s location closed eleven months after opening, a quiet, unremarked failure buried in a corporate quarterly report under "underperforming regional assets." Rosa never mentioned it to her customers, and neither did the sign in her window, which still read, as it had for twenty-two years, simply: OPEN.\n\n'
   '7. She had not out-priced a company with four thousand locations, and she had not out-marketed its regional budget. She had done something a spreadsheet could not easily measure: made twenty-two Tuesdays feel like they belonged to the people who kept showing up, one loaf, one lesson, one remembered coffee order at a time.'))],
 [('What explicit detail does paragraph 1 give about how long Rosa has run her bakery?', 'Twenty-two years, long enough that she baked wedding cakes for couples who now bring their own children into the shop.'),
  ('What explicit threat does paragraph 1 introduce, and what specific competitive advantage does it have?', 'BreadGiant, a national chain with over four thousand locations, opens two blocks away offering loaves at half Rosa\'s price.'),
  ('Compare Rosa\'s and BreadGiant\'s resources as explicitly listed in paragraph 3. What core pattern does this comparison establish?', 'Rosa has twenty-two years of personal relationships and memory; BreadGiant has four thousand locations, a marketing budget, and the ability to sell below cost — this establishes the underdog-versus-giant pattern, an individual facing an overwhelming, resource-rich opponent.'),
  ('Identify the traditional story pattern this modern story draws on, and name the specific ancient story mentioned in the lesson that shares this pattern.', 'The underdog-versus-giant pattern, shared with the story of David and Goliath, where a much weaker figure faces a vastly more powerful opponent.'),
  ('In the traditional pattern, the underdog often wins using an unconventional method rather than matching the opponent\'s own strength. What unconventional method does Rosa use instead of lowering prices or advertising, according to paragraph 4?', 'She starts a free Tuesday night bread-making class, building direct relationships with the community instead of competing on price or marketing.'),
  ('How does Rosa\'s method in paragraph 4 mirror the "unconventional weapon" part of the underdog pattern (like the sling and stone in the original story) while still being entirely new?', 'Like the sling and stone, her method does not match the giant\'s own strength (money, scale) at all; instead of a physical weapon, her "unconventional weapon" is community connection and skill-sharing, rendering the ancient pattern in a completely modern, nonviolent form.'),
  ('What explicit detail from paragraph 5 shows the bread-making classes having a real effect on the neighborhood?', 'A dozen former students started baking their own bread at home, and three began volunteering unpaid to help Rosa during busy mornings.'),
  ('How does the detail of former students becoming unpaid volunteers develop the underdog pattern beyond simply "the small business survived"?', 'It shows the community actively fighting alongside the underdog rather than just passively rooting for her, deepening the pattern into one where the underdog\'s unconventional method turns bystanders into allies.'),
  ('What explicit outcome does paragraph 6 state about BreadGiant\'s location?', 'It closed eleven months after opening, described in a corporate report as an "underperforming regional asset."'),
  ('How does the phrase "quiet, unremarked failure buried in a corporate quarterly report" in paragraph 6 render the traditional giant-defeat scene new, compared to a dramatic physical battle in an ancient story?', 'Instead of a dramatic, visible defeat, BreadGiant\'s loss is bureaucratic and nearly invisible, reflecting how modern institutional "giants" are more likely to fail quietly through spreadsheets and closures than through a single decisive, visible clash.'),
  ('What explicit detail from paragraph 6 shows that Rosa does not treat the outcome as a triumphant victory?', 'She never mentions the closure to her customers, and her shop\'s sign still simply reads "OPEN," as it had for twenty-two years, without any change to mark the win.'),
  ('How does Rosa\'s lack of a triumphant reaction in paragraph 6 depart from how an underdog\'s victory is often portrayed in traditional versions of this story pattern?', 'Traditional underdog stories often end with a clear, celebrated moment of triumph; Rosa\'s quiet, unchanged response reframes victory as continuity rather than conquest, a modern departure from the pattern\'s usual dramatic climax.'),
  ('Explain how paragraph 7\'s final sentence ("made twenty-two Tuesdays feel like they belonged to the people...") redefines what "winning" means in this modern version of the pattern.', 'It redefines winning not as defeating the giant directly, but as strengthening the community bonds the giant could never replicate — a modern, relationship-based version of victory rather than a purely competitive one.'),
  ('Identify at least two specific ways this story "renders new" the traditional underdog-versus-giant pattern, rather than simply retelling it with different character names.', 'It replaces physical combat with community-building as the "weapon," and it replaces a dramatic, publicly celebrated victory with a quiet, almost invisible one (the corporate closure, the unchanged sign) — both choices update the pattern\'s meaning for a modern, non-violent, community-centered context rather than simply relabeling an old story.'),
  ('If this story had ended with Rosa\'s bakery closing instead of BreadGiant\'s, would it still be drawing on the same traditional pattern? Explain your reasoning.', 'It could still reference the same pattern by contrast, showing what happens when the underdog does not find an effective unconventional method, but it would no longer fulfill the pattern\'s traditional meaning of triumph through cleverness or community rather than strength, since that outcome depends on the underdog succeeding.'),
  ('What explicit comparison does paragraph 2 make to explain the regional director\'s belief that Rosa\'s bakery is temporary?', 'He is compared to someone who believes water will eventually fill any container it is poured into "without malice, simply because that is what water does," suggesting he sees BreadGiant\'s expansion as an inevitable, impersonal force rather than a deliberate attack.'),
  ('How does this water comparison in paragraph 2 characterize BreadGiant as a "giant" figure differently than a traditional giant character might be portrayed?', 'Unlike a traditional giant who is often portrayed as menacing or deliberately cruel, BreadGiant\'s giant-like power is portrayed as impersonal and almost mechanical, an update that reflects how modern institutional power can operate without any individual person intending harm.'),
  ('What explicit detail in paragraph 3 explains specifically how BreadGiant could afford to sell bread at half price, something Rosa could not do?', 'It has warehouses full of capital, allowing it to sell below its own cost for a period of time specifically to drive out a competitor.'),
  ('Why is the detail about BreadGiant\'s ability to sell below cost, in paragraph 3, important context for understanding why Rosa could not simply compete on price?', 'It clarifies that Rosa\'s disadvantage is not a lack of effort or skill but a structural financial disadvantage; only a company with vast reserved capital can sustainably lose money on individual sales, which explains why price competition was never a realistic option for her.'),
  ('Explain how the ending detail that Rosa "never mentioned" BreadGiant\'s closure to her customers (paragraph 6) reinforces what her true goal seems to have been throughout the story.', 'It reinforces that her goal was never really to defeat BreadGiant specifically, but to keep serving and connecting with her community; the closure is almost incidental to her, a byproduct of her real focus rather than a victory she needed to announce.')],
 600)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- RI.8.8
add('ri88-evaluating-arguments', 'Should Cedar Falls Ban Single-Use Plastic Bags?', 'RI.8.8',
 'Delineate and evaluate the argument and specific claims in a text, assessing whether the reasoning is sound and the evidence is relevant and sufficient; recognize when irrelevant evidence is introduced.',
 'Delineate the specific claims in an argumentative text, evaluate whether the reasoning connecting evidence to each claim is sound, and recognize when evidence introduced is irrelevant to the claim it is meant to support.',
 'A sound argument has claims directly supported by relevant, sufficient evidence. Evidence is irrelevant when it does not actually address the specific claim it appears next to, even if it is a true and interesting fact. At grade 8, evaluating an argument means checking each individual claim against its evidence, not just judging whether the overall conclusion sounds reasonable.',
 [('An article claims, "Plastic bag bans reduce litter," and supports it with, "After a ban, a coastal cleanup organization reported 38 percent fewer plastic bags collected on local beaches." Is this evidence relevant to the claim?',
   'Yes, this evidence is relevant and directly measures the specific claim (litter reduction) using a directly related statistic (fewer bags found in litter cleanups).'),
  ('The same article also states, "Plastic was first mass-produced in the 1940s, and global plastic production has increased every decade since." Is this evidence relevant to the specific claim that bans reduce local litter?',
   'No, this is irrelevant to that specific claim; while it is a true fact about plastic\'s history, it says nothing about whether a local bag ban actually reduces local litter, so it does not support or weaken the claim.')],
 [('Should Cedar Falls Ban Single-Use Plastic Bags? (Part 1)', (
   '1. The Cedar Falls city council is considering an ordinance banning single-use plastic bags at grocery and retail stores. I support the ban, based on three specific claims: it will reduce local litter, it will save the city money on storm drain maintenance, and it will not meaningfully increase costs for low-income shoppers.\n\n'
   '2. First, the litter claim. Neighboring Fairview banned plastic bags two years ago. A volunteer cleanup organization there reported a 34 percent drop in plastic bags collected during their quarterly creek cleanups, comparing the two years before the ban to the two years after. This is a direct, relevant measurement of the exact outcome the claim predicts: less plastic bag litter after a ban.\n\n'
   '3. Second, the storm drain claim. Cedar Falls\'s own public works department reported spending $214,000 last year clearing plastic debris, primarily bags, from storm drains after major rain events. Fairview\'s public works department, after its ban, reported a 40 percent reduction in debris-related drain clogs specifically attributed to plastic bags. Together, these two figures directly support the claim that a ban could meaningfully reduce Cedar Falls\'s own storm drain costs.')),
  ('Should Cedar Falls Ban Single-Use Plastic Bags? (Part 2)', (
   '4. It is worth noting that plastic bags were introduced to American grocery stores in the 1970s and were marketed at the time as an environmentally friendly alternative to paper bags, since they used less energy to produce. This is a genuinely interesting piece of history, but it has no bearing on whether a ban today would reduce Cedar Falls\'s current litter or drain-clogging problems, and I include it here only as background, not as evidence for or against the ban.\n\n'
   '5. Third, the cost-to-shoppers claim. Fairview\'s ordinance includes a provision allowing shoppers enrolled in state food assistance programs to receive reusable bags free of charge from participating stores, funded by a small per-transaction fee on non-assistance customers. In the two years since, no formal complaints about the provision have been filed with Fairview\'s consumer affairs office. This directly addresses whether the specific claim, that costs to low-income shoppers would not meaningfully increase, holds up in a comparable city.\n\n'
   '6. I want to be clear about the limits of this evidence. Fairview is a similar-sized city with a similar climate and grocery infrastructure to Cedar Falls, which makes its data a reasonable comparison. But it is not Cedar Falls, and local factors — different store layouts, different weather patterns, different shopper habits — could affect how closely Cedar Falls\'s results would match Fairview\'s. The evidence supports the three claims; it does not guarantee identical results.'))],
 [('What are the three specific claims the author states in paragraph 1?', 'That a ban will reduce local litter, save the city money on storm drain maintenance, and not meaningfully increase costs for low-income shoppers.'),
  ('What explicit evidence does paragraph 2 provide for the litter claim, and what specific statistic does it include?', 'Fairview\'s volunteer cleanup organization reported a 34 percent drop in plastic bags collected during quarterly creek cleanups, comparing two years before and after its ban.'),
  ('Evaluate whether the evidence in paragraph 2 is relevant to the litter claim. Explain your reasoning.', 'It is relevant, because it directly measures the exact outcome the claim predicts (less plastic bag litter) using a real before-and-after comparison from a similar situation.'),
  ('What two specific figures does paragraph 3 provide to support the storm drain claim?', 'Cedar Falls spent $214,000 last year clearing plastic debris from storm drains, and Fairview reported a 40 percent reduction in plastic-bag-related drain clogs after its ban.'),
  ('Why does the author present both a Cedar Falls figure and a Fairview figure together in paragraph 3, rather than just one or the other?', 'Together, the Cedar Falls figure establishes the current cost the city faces, and the Fairview figure shows how much a ban could realistically reduce a similar cost, making the combined evidence more directly relevant and sufficient than either figure alone.'),
  ('What piece of evidence does paragraph 4 introduce, and what does the author explicitly say about its relevance?', 'The history of plastic bags being introduced in the 1970s as an "environmentally friendly" alternative to paper; the author explicitly states it "has no bearing" on whether a ban today would help and is included only as background, not as evidence.'),
  ('Why might the author choose to include the irrelevant historical detail in paragraph 4 at all, if it does not support any claim?', 'Including it as clearly labeled background, rather than as supporting evidence, may add context or interest for the reader while the author\'s explicit disclaimer prevents it from being mistaken for actual support of the argument.'),
  ('What explicit provision from Fairview\'s ordinance does paragraph 5 describe, related to low-income shoppers?', 'Shoppers enrolled in state food assistance programs receive reusable bags free of charge, funded by a small per-transaction fee on other customers.'),
  ('What evidence does paragraph 5 provide that this provision has worked as intended, and evaluate whether it is sufficient on its own.', 'No formal complaints about the provision have been filed with Fairview\'s consumer affairs office in two years; this is relevant but may not be fully sufficient on its own, since an absence of formal complaints does not necessarily prove there were no informal difficulties, only that none rose to a formal complaint.'),
  ('What limitation does the author explicitly acknowledge about using Fairview\'s data in paragraph 6?', 'Fairview is not Cedar Falls, and local factors — different store layouts, weather patterns, and shopper habits — could affect how closely Cedar Falls\'s results would match Fairview\'s.'),
  ('How does the author\'s acknowledgment in paragraph 6 affect the overall soundness of the argument, compared to an argument that made no such acknowledgment?', 'It strengthens the argument\'s credibility by being honest about the evidence\'s limits, showing the author supports the claims with real but not absolutely certain evidence, rather than overstating what comparison data from another city can prove.'),
  ('Which piece of evidence in the entire article is explicitly irrelevant to any of the three claims, and how do you know?', 'The 1970s history of plastic bags being marketed as environmentally friendly (paragraph 4) is irrelevant, and the author explicitly states this directly in the text rather than leaving it for the reader to infer.'),
  ('Evaluate whether the evidence provided for the storm drain claim (paragraph 3) is sufficient, or whether additional evidence would strengthen it. Explain your reasoning.', 'The evidence is reasonably sufficient, since it includes both a real local cost figure and a comparable city\'s actual outcome after a similar policy; it could be strengthened further by data from more than one comparison city, but two directly relevant figures already provide a meaningful basis for the claim.'),
  ('A reader claims this article is "biased" because the author openly supports the ban from the first sentence. Using evidence from the text, evaluate whether stating a position upfront makes the argument\'s reasoning unsound.', 'Stating a position upfront does not by itself make reasoning unsound; the article\'s soundness depends on whether its evidence is relevant and sufficient for each claim, which paragraphs 2, 3, and 5 demonstrate, and the author\'s explicit acknowledgment of the irrelevant detail (paragraph 4) and the evidence\'s limitations (paragraph 6) suggest a transparent approach rather than a one-sided, unsound one.'),
  ('If a fourth paragraph were added citing "national plastic production has tripled since 1990" as evidence for the storm drain cost claim specifically, would this be relevant or irrelevant? Explain your reasoning using the standard set by paragraph 4.', 'It would be irrelevant to the storm drain cost claim specifically, using the same reasoning the author applied to the 1970s history in paragraph 4 — a true, general fact about plastic production nationally says nothing about Cedar Falls\'s specific storm drain costs or how a local ban would affect them.'),
  ('What explicit time period does paragraph 2 use for its before-and-after comparison of Fairview\'s cleanup data?', 'Two years before the ban compared to two years after the ban.'),
  ('Why is comparing the same two-year window before and after the ban (paragraph 2) a stronger form of evidence than a single after-the-fact number alone would be?', 'A before-and-after comparison over the same length of time helps isolate the effect of the ban itself, making it more likely the 34 percent drop is connected to the policy rather than to some unrelated yearly fluctuation.'),
  ('What explicit dollar figure does paragraph 3 give for Cedar Falls\'s current storm drain cleaning costs?', '$214,000 spent last year clearing plastic debris, primarily bags, from storm drains after major rain events.'),
  ('Delineate the specific claim paragraph 5 is meant to support, and evaluate whether the evidence provided directly measures that claim or only partially addresses it.', 'The claim is that the ban will not meaningfully increase costs for low-income shoppers; the evidence (no formal complaints in two years) partially addresses this by suggesting no major problems arose, but does not directly measure actual costs shoppers may have absorbed informally, so it only partially, not fully, measures the specific claim.'),
  ('Why does the author\'s own acknowledgment of uncertainty in paragraph 6 make the argument more sound rather than less sound, even though it admits the evidence is not perfect?', 'Sound reasoning includes recognizing the actual limits of one\'s evidence rather than overstating it; by honestly noting that Fairview\'s results may not perfectly predict Cedar Falls\'s results, the author avoids an overreaching claim the evidence cannot fully support, which is a mark of careful, sound reasoning rather than weak reasoning.')],
 600)
